WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.625
&gt;&gt; We'll get started.

00:00:05.626 --> 00:00:08.010
So, this is the session
on Blockchains,

00:00:08.011 --> 00:00:09.930
so welcome to this session
on Blockchains.

00:00:09.931 --> 00:00:13.175
So, we have a bunch of interesting
talks in this session.

00:00:13.176 --> 00:00:16.450
Our first presentation
will be on a work that

00:00:16.451 --> 00:00:19.890
marries traditional
databases with Blockchain.

00:00:19.891 --> 00:00:23.880
The second work would be
on exploring how we can

00:00:23.881 --> 00:00:26.600
use computation verification

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:27.910
in the setting of Blockchain.

00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:29.570
Then finally we have another work

00:00:29.571 --> 00:00:30.990
that explores how we can use

00:00:30.991 --> 00:00:32.770
enclaves to kind of achieve

00:00:32.771 --> 00:00:34.340
better performance
and confidentiality

00:00:34.341 --> 00:00:36.510
in the context of Blockchains.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.835
So, the first speaker
is going to be

00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:42.770
Professor Carsten Binnig from

00:00:42.771 --> 00:00:45.090
Technical University
of Darmstadt.

00:00:45.091 --> 00:00:47.795
He's also an adjunct professor at

00:00:47.796 --> 00:00:49.760
Brown University and before

00:00:49.761 --> 00:00:51.945
that he was an architect
at SAP HANA.

00:00:51.946 --> 00:00:54.095
He's a recipient of

00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:56.510
Google Faculty Award and he has

00:00:56.511 --> 00:00:58.620
a bunch of Best Paper
Awards at IEEE,

00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:00.250
Big Data and VLDB and

00:01:00.251 --> 00:01:02.940
SIGMOD and he's been
recently exploring

00:01:02.941 --> 00:01:06.000
the overlap between Blockchains

00:01:06.001 --> 00:01:09.285
and databases and he's
going to talk about that.

00:01:09.286 --> 00:01:11.945
&gt;&gt; Wonderful. Good.
Do you hear me?

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:14.185
So, the mic should be on.

00:01:14.186 --> 00:01:17.430
So, today, as Arvind mentioned,

00:01:17.431 --> 00:01:22.755
I'm talking about a database
view on Blockchains.

00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:26.140
So, my main research is
actually, in general,

00:01:26.141 --> 00:01:28.485
in distributed
databases, especially

00:01:28.486 --> 00:01:31.215
distributed databases
together with networks.

00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:32.975
So, I'm a database person.

00:01:32.976 --> 00:01:37.620
Just to give you a quick
introduction where I come from.

00:01:37.621 --> 00:01:41.240
So, why I got interested
in Blockchains is

00:01:41.241 --> 00:01:43.310
because people are not using

00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:45.515
Blockchains for
cryptocurrencies only,

00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:47.630
but they use Blockchains
more and more

00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:50.720
for general shared databases,

00:01:50.721 --> 00:01:53.060
to write data into from

00:01:53.061 --> 00:01:54.575
one trusted domain and

00:01:54.576 --> 00:01:57.030
access it from
another trusted domain.

00:01:57.031 --> 00:02:00.080
The main reason why
people are using it as

00:02:00.081 --> 00:02:03.530
a platform for data sharing is,

00:02:03.531 --> 00:02:05.200
there are many different reasons.

00:02:05.201 --> 00:02:08.730
One is because it keeps
the history of all transactions.

00:02:08.731 --> 00:02:11.329
Meaning, if something is
wrong in your database,

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.980
you can go back and still
look at what went wrong.

00:02:14.981 --> 00:02:16.595
In some states in the USA,

00:02:16.596 --> 00:02:19.850
they even changed the laws
that blockhains or

00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:23.750
the data in Blockchain counts
as evidence in court,

00:02:23.751 --> 00:02:26.330
which is a strong reason
if you share data

00:02:26.331 --> 00:02:30.055
between different
trusted domains.

00:02:30.056 --> 00:02:33.410
The second one is why
it's interesting is

00:02:33.411 --> 00:02:36.340
also because no tampering
is possible after-the-fact.

00:02:36.341 --> 00:02:37.700
So, once the transaction is in,

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.860
you cannot modify the list of

00:02:39.861 --> 00:02:42.675
transactions you store
in your Blockchain.

00:02:42.676 --> 00:02:45.660
Even more interesting, you
don't need a trusted authority

00:02:45.661 --> 00:02:48.120
for making all these decisions.

00:02:48.121 --> 00:02:51.200
So, the machinery and Blockchain
is designed in order to

00:02:51.201 --> 00:02:55.145
give you the two properties,

00:02:55.146 --> 00:02:57.740
that it stores all transactions
and it doesn't allow

00:02:57.741 --> 00:03:00.940
tampering without having
a trusted authority.

00:03:00.941 --> 00:03:02.870
So, and you see

00:03:02.871 --> 00:03:06.170
that it's actually used as
shared databases and there are

00:03:06.171 --> 00:03:08.745
many applications outside that

00:03:08.746 --> 00:03:09.770
try actually to use

00:03:09.771 --> 00:03:12.085
Blockchains already
as a shared database.

00:03:12.086 --> 00:03:15.120
They range from different
or cover different domains,

00:03:15.121 --> 00:03:18.410
not data, just in from
health institutions,

00:03:18.411 --> 00:03:20.600
but also supply chains where
you have different entities,

00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:22.520
the supplier and the manufacturer

00:03:22.521 --> 00:03:24.820
requiring to access
the same database.

00:03:24.821 --> 00:03:26.870
But also to other use
cases where you want to

00:03:26.871 --> 00:03:28.910
have decentralized
copyright management.

00:03:28.911 --> 00:03:31.410
So, for example, Binded is

00:03:31.411 --> 00:03:33.240
a copyright management
platform for

00:03:33.241 --> 00:03:35.195
images where you
put in the image,

00:03:35.196 --> 00:03:38.630
it gets a timestamp and
thus it has the copyright,

00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:40.385
and if somebody else at the end

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.130
wants to upload
another similar image,

00:03:43.131 --> 00:03:44.630
you have to prove
that the other image

00:03:44.631 --> 00:03:46.240
was in the database before.

00:03:46.241 --> 00:03:49.515
So, the question is
now, are we done now?

00:03:49.516 --> 00:03:51.320
Is this the end of the talk?

00:03:51.321 --> 00:03:54.010
So, are existing
Blockchain systems,

00:03:54.011 --> 00:03:55.760
is they are good
enough for supporting

00:03:55.761 --> 00:03:57.740
all the different applications
that people have in

00:03:57.741 --> 00:04:02.060
mind and when they want to use
them as a shared database?

00:04:02.061 --> 00:04:05.095
So, what I'm going to do
in the talk is, therefore,

00:04:05.096 --> 00:04:07.220
I'm first going quickly- I think

00:04:07.221 --> 00:04:09.580
most of the people might
know the Blockchain basics,

00:04:09.581 --> 00:04:12.410
but I'll still go quickly over
the Blockchain background,

00:04:12.411 --> 00:04:14.990
some basic terms that I'm not

00:04:14.991 --> 00:04:16.490
talking about things that are

00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:18.235
out of context in
the rest of the talk,

00:04:18.236 --> 00:04:19.910
then talk about the challenges

00:04:19.911 --> 00:04:21.910
why Blockchains, in my eyes,

00:04:21.911 --> 00:04:24.050
are not sufficient
yet to use them as

00:04:24.051 --> 00:04:26.930
a shared database and
then go towards what

00:04:26.931 --> 00:04:28.820
we've been doing in the past year

00:04:28.821 --> 00:04:30.380
with Blockchain to be developing

00:04:30.381 --> 00:04:32.930
a shared or Database
System on top of

00:04:32.931 --> 00:04:34.280
Blockchains that mitigate some

00:04:34.281 --> 00:04:35.625
of the problems that we've seen,

00:04:35.626 --> 00:04:38.585
or that I am going
to discuss before.

00:04:38.586 --> 00:04:41.520
So, for Blockchains,
just the quick overview,

00:04:41.521 --> 00:04:43.370
I don't want to
spend too much time.

00:04:43.371 --> 00:04:46.500
So, Blockchains
the basis they use,

00:04:46.501 --> 00:04:49.330
a tamper-proof ledger
to store data,

00:04:49.331 --> 00:04:50.970
which I already
mentioned, it's a list of

00:04:50.971 --> 00:04:53.095
transactions, it's append only.

00:04:53.096 --> 00:04:54.890
The transactions are
appended in blocks,

00:04:54.891 --> 00:04:56.265
so if new data is written,

00:04:56.266 --> 00:04:58.370
the new data is appended or mined

00:04:58.371 --> 00:05:01.360
in blocks and the ledger
is fully replicated.

00:05:01.361 --> 00:05:03.000
Here you see already,

00:05:03.001 --> 00:05:05.005
if something is mined or

00:05:05.006 --> 00:05:07.400
appended in blocks and
it's fully replicated,

00:05:07.401 --> 00:05:09.440
there seems to be a lot
of inefficiency in

00:05:09.441 --> 00:05:11.490
the system itself and this is,

00:05:11.491 --> 00:05:13.640
again, one of the challenges
that you tackle when you

00:05:13.641 --> 00:05:16.910
want to use it as a
general-purpose Database System.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:23.900
So, why it's so interesting
as a shared database,

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:25.310
I already mentioned is, if

00:05:25.311 --> 00:05:27.080
an update happened to
the shared database,

00:05:27.081 --> 00:05:30.230
which is replicated to
all the peers in the Blockchain,

00:05:30.231 --> 00:05:33.080
then everybody or
at least the majority

00:05:33.081 --> 00:05:34.830
depending on what protocol you're

00:05:34.831 --> 00:05:36.590
using is to agree on

00:05:36.591 --> 00:05:39.870
the appends and there are
different ways to assure that,

00:05:39.871 --> 00:05:41.510
nevermind what you
are using, if you're

00:05:41.511 --> 00:05:43.490
using a public
or private Blockchain.

00:05:43.491 --> 00:05:48.335
That's what also we will
go over in a few seconds.

00:05:48.336 --> 00:05:51.290
The last bit of piece
of information that

00:05:51.291 --> 00:05:53.750
I'd like to introduce is
the term smart contracts,

00:05:53.751 --> 00:05:55.970
which are nothing else
than trusted procedures

00:05:55.971 --> 00:05:58.340
that are stored in the
Blockchain that can modify data.

00:05:58.341 --> 00:06:01.950
It's your custom logic that
you deploy in a Blockchain.

00:06:01.951 --> 00:06:03.980
For database person, it
would be something like

00:06:03.981 --> 00:06:07.370
a stored procedure that can
be called from outside.

00:06:07.371 --> 00:06:09.500
But for smart contracts,

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:11.930
the specific thing is
that all the participants

00:06:11.931 --> 00:06:15.630
agree on the same code if
you're on the Blockchain.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:23.055
The second slide, just to
get the background right,

00:06:23.056 --> 00:06:25.710
there are two platforms

00:06:25.711 --> 00:06:28.375
or ways how Blockchains
are deployed.

00:06:28.376 --> 00:06:30.050
There are public Blockchains,

00:06:30.051 --> 00:06:32.490
means that everybody,
every participant,

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:35.450
can join the Blockchain and

00:06:35.451 --> 00:06:38.945
they usually use more expensive
consensus protocols.

00:06:38.946 --> 00:06:41.820
I won't go into
details for those,

00:06:41.821 --> 00:06:45.750
but just to make sure that
we're on the same side,

00:06:45.751 --> 00:06:47.190
there are also
private Blockchains,

00:06:47.191 --> 00:06:51.230
which target a different type
of application where not

00:06:51.231 --> 00:06:54.020
everybody is allowed to

00:06:54.021 --> 00:06:56.905
join the Blockchain but
it's more limited set,

00:06:56.906 --> 00:06:58.340
targeted towards a limited set of

00:06:58.341 --> 00:06:59.860
participants that
know each other.

00:06:59.861 --> 00:07:01.670
Therefore, they use a bit of

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:04.430
less expensive consensus
protocols and this

00:07:04.431 --> 00:07:07.130
already increases
the performance a bit when

00:07:07.131 --> 00:07:10.160
you use Blockchains
as a Database System.

00:07:10.161 --> 00:07:11.650
So, the question is,

00:07:11.651 --> 00:07:13.640
that's where I'd actually
like to start my talk,

00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:15.500
what are the challenges
if you want to use

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:19.630
a Blockchains as
a shared Database System?

00:07:19.631 --> 00:07:22.315
The main challenge, I
already mentioned that,

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:26.310
is clearly the performance
of Blockchains.

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:29.780
There was a paper last year
on SIGMOD where they analyzed

00:07:29.781 --> 00:07:31.819
the throughput and the latency

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.965
of different Blockchain systems.

00:07:33.966 --> 00:07:36.730
One is Ethereum, that
is pretty well-known,

00:07:36.731 --> 00:07:38.960
which you can set up as
a public but also as

00:07:38.961 --> 00:07:41.850
a private Blockchain,
as well as Hyperledger,

00:07:41.851 --> 00:07:47.385
which is the IBM version
of Blockchain system.

00:07:47.386 --> 00:07:49.070
But what you see here is

00:07:49.071 --> 00:07:51.110
that the throughput, for example,

00:07:51.111 --> 00:07:52.790
for a classical
database benchmarks,

00:07:52.791 --> 00:07:54.930
so if you use the Blockchains as

00:07:54.931 --> 00:07:57.495
a key-value store,
it's pretty low.

00:07:57.496 --> 00:08:00.710
So, they reported
around, for Ethereum,

00:08:00.711 --> 00:08:04.355
around 300 transactions per
second that can be executed.

00:08:04.356 --> 00:08:06.065
If you look carefully
in the paper,

00:08:06.066 --> 00:08:07.900
this is not the average
transaction rate,

00:08:07.901 --> 00:08:09.070
that's the peak transaction rate.

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:11.435
So, the average transaction rate

00:08:11.436 --> 00:08:13.470
is actually much, much lower.

00:08:13.471 --> 00:08:15.930
So, clearly here you see that

00:08:15.931 --> 00:08:19.470
the transaction and
the performance that you get,

00:08:19.471 --> 00:08:21.500
the transaction rate,
the throughput

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:22.850
and the latency
that you get out of

00:08:22.851 --> 00:08:24.365
these Blockchains is far from

00:08:24.366 --> 00:08:26.845
what typical
applications need today.

00:08:26.846 --> 00:08:28.805
Even, for example, if you look at

00:08:28.806 --> 00:08:31.325
what Visa is
processing on average,

00:08:31.326 --> 00:08:33.165
so Visa transactions are

00:08:33.166 --> 00:08:35.855
around 2,000
transactions per second.

00:08:35.856 --> 00:08:37.995
If you look into the applications

00:08:37.996 --> 00:08:40.685
how people want to
use Blockchains,

00:08:40.686 --> 00:08:44.300
they also want to store
sensor data in the Blockchain,

00:08:44.301 --> 00:08:47.910
which even require much higher
transaction rates.

00:08:47.911 --> 00:08:52.355
So, the performance
itself is one problem.

00:08:52.356 --> 00:08:55.700
The second one is, if you today

00:08:55.701 --> 00:08:57.680
want to use one of
the Blockchain systems

00:08:57.681 --> 00:08:59.355
to set up a shared database,

00:08:59.356 --> 00:09:01.710
you are confronted with the
"zoo" of those systems,

00:09:01.711 --> 00:09:04.280
and it's really not
clear which one to use.

00:09:04.281 --> 00:09:05.330
They come, (a) with

00:09:05.331 --> 00:09:07.315
different programming
and execution models,

00:09:07.316 --> 00:09:13.320
and the question is with

00:09:13.321 --> 00:09:15.455
which platform are you able
to implement your logic

00:09:15.456 --> 00:09:17.840
that you want to have
for the shared database.

00:09:17.841 --> 00:09:19.490
Where can you implement? In which

00:09:19.491 --> 00:09:21.680
can you implement transactions or

00:09:21.681 --> 00:09:26.035
the transaction logic that
is needed for use case.

00:09:26.036 --> 00:09:29.870
Even worse, because there's
so much development going on,

00:09:29.871 --> 00:09:33.580
you don't know what is
the best Blockchain

00:09:33.581 --> 00:09:35.125
for your workload and

00:09:35.126 --> 00:09:37.640
(b) which ones will
survive at the end,

00:09:37.641 --> 00:09:40.895
because there's
so much movement in the sector

00:09:40.896 --> 00:09:44.720
and people are trying to
come up with optimizations,

00:09:44.721 --> 00:09:46.505
new platforms and it's unclear

00:09:46.506 --> 00:09:48.230
which one you should

00:09:48.231 --> 00:09:50.620
build your shared
database application on.

00:09:50.621 --> 00:09:55.230
The last one is even more
severe, that Blockchains,

00:09:55.231 --> 00:09:57.260
you might assume if you deploy

00:09:57.261 --> 00:09:59.825
your code in the
Blockchain and run it,

00:09:59.826 --> 00:10:01.325
you get some kind- because

00:10:01.326 --> 00:10:04.010
even procedures run through
the consensus mechanism that

00:10:04.011 --> 00:10:05.300
multiple nodes need to execute

00:10:05.301 --> 00:10:07.210
the same code bases
and at the end

00:10:07.211 --> 00:10:09.240
the output of
the procedure is verified,

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:11.985
so meaning if
all the outputs are equal.

00:10:11.986 --> 00:10:14.090
But that's not true
for purely read

00:10:14.091 --> 00:10:16.130
only functions that you would

00:10:16.131 --> 00:10:18.270
implement in your smart contract.

00:10:18.271 --> 00:10:20.830
They are only executed by
one peer in the Blockchain.

00:10:20.831 --> 00:10:22.505
Meaning, if that one peer,

00:10:22.506 --> 00:10:23.660
if that one node,

00:10:23.661 --> 00:10:25.430
is malicious and you
are asking or you're

00:10:25.431 --> 00:10:27.695
sending a read request
to that one peer,

00:10:27.696 --> 00:10:31.060
you might get a wrong value
from the peer end.

00:10:31.061 --> 00:10:35.710
So, the guarantees we are
getting for reads, for example,

00:10:35.711 --> 00:10:38.660
is not enough for

00:10:38.661 --> 00:10:40.430
many of the shared database
where you want to know,

00:10:40.431 --> 00:10:41.930
is the value I've
been reading actually

00:10:41.931 --> 00:10:43.980
the value that is
stored in the database?

00:10:43.981 --> 00:10:46.820
So, there are
many guarantees that you

00:10:46.821 --> 00:10:47.990
actually then want to add to

00:10:47.991 --> 00:10:49.750
your Database System or to your,

00:10:49.751 --> 00:10:52.185
if you use your Blockchain
as a shared database,

00:10:52.186 --> 00:10:55.910
and what we think would be

00:10:55.911 --> 00:10:57.500
nice is if you could

00:10:57.501 --> 00:10:59.690
execute transactions on
a shared Blockchain,

00:10:59.691 --> 00:11:02.500
at the end verify if the
execution of the transaction,

00:11:02.501 --> 00:11:04.535
meaning if all your read and
write operations

00:11:04.536 --> 00:11:05.780
was correct with

00:11:05.781 --> 00:11:06.890
regard to the guarantees what

00:11:06.891 --> 00:11:09.290
the database wants to give you,

00:11:09.291 --> 00:11:11.995
and if you discover that
a violation happened,

00:11:11.996 --> 00:11:15.140
that you can go back to
the last verified checkpoint.

00:11:15.141 --> 00:11:18.020
So, the ideas of
transactional theory more

00:11:18.021 --> 00:11:20.630
into implemented in Blockchains,

00:11:20.631 --> 00:11:23.015
but also transactions
that are verifiable.

00:11:23.016 --> 00:11:25.965
So, how are we going
to achieve this?

00:11:25.966 --> 00:11:28.095
This is the idea of BlockchainDB.

00:11:28.096 --> 00:11:29.840
The idea is that we built

00:11:29.841 --> 00:11:32.555
a Middleware on top
of Blockchains.

00:11:32.556 --> 00:11:35.650
The idea is what
the BlockchainDB gives

00:11:35.651 --> 00:11:39.180
us is that it gives
us a unified API.

00:11:39.181 --> 00:11:41.340
So, we tackle the problem

00:11:41.341 --> 00:11:43.040
of having a sue of those systems

00:11:43.041 --> 00:11:45.350
by having a unified front-end

00:11:45.351 --> 00:11:47.210
and an exchangeable back-end,

00:11:47.211 --> 00:11:48.915
similar to what MySQL is today.

00:11:48.916 --> 00:11:52.375
For databases, you get
a unified front-end and you

00:11:52.376 --> 00:11:56.410
can plug in your storage
engine that you like to have,

00:11:56.411 --> 00:11:58.915
which gives you the
performance characteristics

00:11:58.916 --> 00:12:02.825
maybe that you want to
have for your application.

00:12:02.826 --> 00:12:04.990
The second one is
that we can apply

00:12:04.991 --> 00:12:07.450
typical database optimizations
in the Middleware.

00:12:07.451 --> 00:12:09.455
So, we've seen that

00:12:09.456 --> 00:12:12.575
the performance itself
is a major limitation,

00:12:12.576 --> 00:12:14.600
and by applying some tricks
in the Middleware,

00:12:14.601 --> 00:12:18.150
by sharding across
certain Blockchains,

00:12:18.151 --> 00:12:20.475
we can achieve much higher
throughput than

00:12:20.476 --> 00:12:23.905
what the existing Blockchains
give us already.

00:12:23.906 --> 00:12:26.205
The last one is that
we want to support

00:12:26.206 --> 00:12:28.910
this notion of
verifiable transactions.

00:12:28.911 --> 00:12:32.205
So, executing read/write
operations via the BlockchainDB,

00:12:32.206 --> 00:12:34.450
and then at the end getting a

00:12:34.451 --> 00:12:36.270
verification mechanism
that tells us

00:12:36.271 --> 00:12:38.720
is a transaction that we've

00:12:38.721 --> 00:12:40.340
executed been correct with

00:12:40.341 --> 00:12:41.620
regard to the consistency level,

00:12:41.621 --> 00:12:44.265
for example, selected
in the Database System?

00:12:44.266 --> 00:12:46.680
Now you could argue,
"Okay, a BlockchainDB,

00:12:46.681 --> 00:12:48.265
this is the component that runs

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:50.450
outside of your Blockchain.

00:12:50.451 --> 00:12:51.835
So, it's an untrusted component,

00:12:51.836 --> 00:12:53.260
doesn't it make things harder?"

00:12:53.261 --> 00:12:54.875
I'll come to that at the end

00:12:54.876 --> 00:12:57.140
and I'll talk a bit about how

00:12:57.141 --> 00:12:59.385
we deal with verification

00:12:59.386 --> 00:13:02.055
with a new component that
is actually untrusted.

00:13:02.056 --> 00:13:06.215
So, what we've
actually been building

00:13:06.216 --> 00:13:10.060
in the last year is not
a full Database System,

00:13:10.061 --> 00:13:11.615
but as a first step,

00:13:11.616 --> 00:13:14.720
we started to build
a Middleware that has

00:13:14.721 --> 00:13:19.040
a simple put/get interface
because this is already from,

00:13:19.041 --> 00:13:21.950
as a basic building block,

00:13:21.951 --> 00:13:24.030
we think we can use later on to

00:13:24.031 --> 00:13:26.275
build transactions on top, and b,

00:13:26.276 --> 00:13:27.400
it gives us already proof of

00:13:27.401 --> 00:13:28.980
concept of how far can we get

00:13:28.981 --> 00:13:30.380
with transaction rates
if you have

00:13:30.381 --> 00:13:32.445
just a simple put/get workload.

00:13:32.446 --> 00:13:34.255
So, the idea is we have

00:13:34.256 --> 00:13:36.700
a Blockchain key-value
store that supports

00:13:36.701 --> 00:13:39.230
at the beginning only the simple

00:13:39.231 --> 00:13:40.615
put/get operations
that we have here and

00:13:40.616 --> 00:13:42.650
we have pluggable back-ends.

00:13:42.651 --> 00:13:45.715
Inside the Blockchain,
in order to achieve

00:13:45.716 --> 00:13:47.245
the higher performance and

00:13:47.246 --> 00:13:49.625
implement some database
optimizations,

00:13:49.626 --> 00:13:51.520
we selected a bunch of techniques

00:13:51.521 --> 00:13:53.340
that are implemented
in the Middleware,

00:13:53.341 --> 00:13:55.035
be it from sharding,

00:13:55.036 --> 00:13:57.470
the data into
multiple Blockchains,

00:13:57.471 --> 00:13:58.770
so not using just one.

00:13:58.771 --> 00:14:01.310
This is a thing that already
some of the Blockchains

00:14:01.311 --> 00:14:04.375
do by now inside the
Blockchain system,

00:14:04.376 --> 00:14:05.830
they support sharding but

00:14:05.831 --> 00:14:07.330
not all of the systems
support it.

00:14:07.331 --> 00:14:11.540
So, what we can do is just
put that on top and support,

00:14:11.541 --> 00:14:14.380
that way some
data-parallel execution

00:14:14.381 --> 00:14:15.810
across Blockchains because if you

00:14:15.811 --> 00:14:17.770
look a bit deeper into
the execution models of

00:14:17.771 --> 00:14:20.115
Blockchains they are to
be fully serialized,

00:14:20.116 --> 00:14:21.850
a serialized execution with

00:14:21.851 --> 00:14:24.730
no parallel execution
of transactions at all,

00:14:24.731 --> 00:14:28.380
and the sharding gives us
some way of parallelizing.

00:14:28.381 --> 00:14:31.645
We implemented a bunch
of other optimizations.

00:14:31.646 --> 00:14:33.280
I want to go just in two of

00:14:33.281 --> 00:14:36.025
them and show you quickly
what the performance and

00:14:36.026 --> 00:14:37.915
impacts are if you

00:14:37.916 --> 00:14:41.640
do that or if you implement
those in the Middleware.

00:14:41.641 --> 00:14:43.840
The first one is, so

00:14:43.841 --> 00:14:46.160
the question is if you
provide a put/get interface,

00:14:46.161 --> 00:14:50.895
what consistency can be provided
if you use a Blockchain?

00:14:50.896 --> 00:14:53.400
In Blockchain key-value store,

00:14:53.401 --> 00:14:56.930
we support different notions
of consistency levels

00:14:56.931 --> 00:14:58.825
or clients like
consistency levels

00:14:58.826 --> 00:15:00.910
that are more expensive
or less expensive.

00:15:00.911 --> 00:15:04.005
The idea is that if
you give up some of

00:15:04.006 --> 00:15:08.055
the consistency similar to
what normal databases also do,

00:15:08.056 --> 00:15:09.615
you can get higher
performance even

00:15:09.616 --> 00:15:11.880
when we use a Blockchain
as a back-end.

00:15:11.881 --> 00:15:14.160
The idea here is that if you

00:15:14.161 --> 00:15:16.190
use a higher consistency
level, for example,

00:15:16.191 --> 00:15:18.290
read your own writes, what

00:15:18.291 --> 00:15:20.460
we do in the Middleware
if a write is coming in,

00:15:20.461 --> 00:15:21.765
if a put is coming in,

00:15:21.766 --> 00:15:25.360
it's first submitted to
the Blockchain but we

00:15:25.361 --> 00:15:29.005
don't wait for the transaction
actually committed,

00:15:29.006 --> 00:15:31.035
but only that it's validated.

00:15:31.036 --> 00:15:33.660
After that, we just append it to

00:15:33.661 --> 00:15:36.965
a list in our Middleware,

00:15:36.966 --> 00:15:40.655
meaning that once a read is
coming in for the same key,

00:15:40.656 --> 00:15:42.370
at that moment we do lazy

00:15:42.371 --> 00:15:44.620
blocking on the
pending transaction

00:15:44.621 --> 00:15:46.450
and wait for
the transaction to be

00:15:46.451 --> 00:15:48.955
pre-committed before we
give back the value.

00:15:48.956 --> 00:15:52.010
So, this is one
technique where you

00:15:52.011 --> 00:15:53.560
already don't need to wait

00:15:53.561 --> 00:15:56.030
for the full execution
of a transaction,

00:15:56.031 --> 00:16:00.375
but you still get
a guaranteed consistency level

00:16:00.376 --> 00:16:02.300
which many of the
applications desire.

00:16:02.301 --> 00:16:04.800
Or you can even go
lower and provide

00:16:04.801 --> 00:16:07.400
only eventual consistency and

00:16:07.401 --> 00:16:09.605
even don't wait for
the transaction

00:16:09.606 --> 00:16:10.730
to be committed and execute,

00:16:10.731 --> 00:16:13.270
just read operations completely
on the status you are

00:16:13.271 --> 00:16:14.710
getting and monitor if

00:16:14.711 --> 00:16:16.440
these transactions
are finally commit,

00:16:16.441 --> 00:16:19.030
so if you can still
guarantee liveliness.

00:16:19.031 --> 00:16:22.940
What you see here is
that if you implement

00:16:22.941 --> 00:16:28.520
just on using a 50/50
workloads of 50 reads,

00:16:28.521 --> 00:16:31.915
50 percent writes, and
Ethereum as a back-end.

00:16:31.916 --> 00:16:35.700
If you want to provide read
your own writes semantics,

00:16:35.701 --> 00:16:37.170
you get only throughput of

00:16:37.171 --> 00:16:39.555
20 put/get operations
at a second,

00:16:39.556 --> 00:16:45.200
and this is in line with
a block bench numbers.

00:16:45.201 --> 00:16:46.580
If you look a bit closer,

00:16:46.581 --> 00:16:49.480
they reported
only numbers as I said

00:16:49.481 --> 00:16:53.025
for max throughput and not
for average throughput.

00:16:53.026 --> 00:16:56.155
See, the eventual consistency

00:16:56.156 --> 00:16:59.005
gives you already a boost
of a factor of five in

00:16:59.006 --> 00:17:01.345
terms of transactions
which is not

00:17:01.346 --> 00:17:05.020
still there what many of
the application needs,

00:17:05.021 --> 00:17:09.485
but it's already a way of
getting higher performance

00:17:09.486 --> 00:17:11.615
out of your Blockchain system if

00:17:11.616 --> 00:17:14.205
you decrease
the consistency level.

00:17:14.206 --> 00:17:15.970
A second technique that
actually is helping

00:17:15.971 --> 00:17:17.730
much more is that,

00:17:17.731 --> 00:17:20.700
in our Middleware we implemented
some form of batching.

00:17:20.701 --> 00:17:22.750
So, we don't submit puts one by

00:17:22.751 --> 00:17:25.040
one to the Blockchain
because the pair

00:17:25.041 --> 00:17:26.730
of transaction overhead
of validating

00:17:26.731 --> 00:17:29.530
transactions is pretty high.

00:17:29.531 --> 00:17:32.370
So meaning, if you
batch them together,

00:17:32.371 --> 00:17:34.180
so multiple puts
in one Blockchain

00:17:34.181 --> 00:17:36.350
transaction, you can get,

00:17:36.351 --> 00:17:39.590
for example, if you block
up to 500 puts in one call,

00:17:39.591 --> 00:17:43.885
the transaction rate of 1,200
transactions per second.

00:17:43.886 --> 00:17:48.020
After that, the
maximum message size

00:17:48.021 --> 00:17:50.630
we used here four byte
integers is exceeded,

00:17:50.631 --> 00:17:54.395
so that's the limit of
batching that you can use.

00:17:54.396 --> 00:18:01.225
So, the plain database
tricks is one thing,

00:18:01.226 --> 00:18:02.440
but the question is now,

00:18:02.441 --> 00:18:06.845
I mentioned a third point
which is important,

00:18:06.846 --> 00:18:11.380
that is getting verifiability
of your operations.

00:18:11.381 --> 00:18:13.375
This is something that we also

00:18:13.376 --> 00:18:18.565
implemented inside Blockchain
or inside our system.

00:18:18.566 --> 00:18:22.230
The idea of verifiable
consistency is that

00:18:22.231 --> 00:18:25.670
a client can verify the
correctness of all operations,

00:18:25.671 --> 00:18:28.240
meaning if the puts
and gets that he

00:18:28.241 --> 00:18:31.270
executed that he sees

00:18:31.271 --> 00:18:32.420
or that he executed

00:18:32.421 --> 00:18:34.345
adhere to the selected
consistency level.

00:18:34.346 --> 00:18:36.280
So, what does it mean for
eventual consistency?

00:18:36.281 --> 00:18:38.920
It means for the
eventual consistency

00:18:38.921 --> 00:18:41.165
that the client doesn't
see any fake reads.

00:18:41.166 --> 00:18:45.680
So, if you think about a
scenario where, for example,

00:18:45.681 --> 00:18:47.970
the Blockchain
Middleware or one of

00:18:47.971 --> 00:18:50.170
the peers is malicious,

00:18:50.171 --> 00:18:51.500
he might return
just the value that

00:18:51.501 --> 00:18:53.000
is not stored in your database

00:18:53.001 --> 00:18:56.210
and we want to be able to
detect it after the fact.

00:18:56.211 --> 00:18:58.700
If you use a Blockchain system
out of the box,

00:18:58.701 --> 00:19:00.295
you don't get that guarantee.

00:19:00.296 --> 00:19:02.760
The second one is
that we want to,

00:19:02.761 --> 00:19:04.365
for example, our
eventual consistency,

00:19:04.366 --> 00:19:05.620
we will also want to
support life in there.

00:19:05.621 --> 00:19:07.030
So, we want to assure that

00:19:07.031 --> 00:19:08.570
all the puts that
I executed through

00:19:08.571 --> 00:19:11.920
BlockchainDB are actually stored

00:19:11.921 --> 00:19:13.140
in the Blockchain system and not

00:19:13.141 --> 00:19:15.645
dropped again by one
of the components.

00:19:15.646 --> 00:19:19.695
So, the problem is
that as I mentioned,

00:19:19.696 --> 00:19:21.700
that all these operations or

00:19:21.701 --> 00:19:24.015
these guarantees could
be violated because,

00:19:24.016 --> 00:19:27.510
in our setup, we have
multiple untrusted components.

00:19:27.511 --> 00:19:29.800
One is our Blockchain
key value store

00:19:29.801 --> 00:19:32.040
that we introduced
as a Middleware.

00:19:32.041 --> 00:19:35.270
But there's also
the peers themselves,

00:19:35.271 --> 00:19:37.565
if you look at them individually,
they are untrusted.

00:19:37.566 --> 00:19:41.630
Meaning, if you just go
to one of the peers,

00:19:41.631 --> 00:19:44.050
we might get the problem
of fake reads,

00:19:44.051 --> 00:19:45.960
and reads are actually
executed by only one peer.

00:19:45.961 --> 00:19:48.345
So, it's a problem of detecting

00:19:48.346 --> 00:19:50.920
if you get a fake read or if,

00:19:50.921 --> 00:19:53.320
for example, the
Blockchain Middleware or

00:19:53.321 --> 00:19:56.975
the peer drops a transaction
and doesn't execute it.

00:19:56.976 --> 00:20:00.640
How we do it is so here
just a high-level overview is

00:20:00.641 --> 00:20:04.225
the idea is that we use
deferred verification mechanism

00:20:04.226 --> 00:20:07.240
where every put that
is executed through

00:20:07.241 --> 00:20:09.295
the key value store updates

00:20:09.296 --> 00:20:13.200
the write set in the
Blockchain itself,

00:20:13.201 --> 00:20:18.700
and the clients additionally
lock their actions,

00:20:18.701 --> 00:20:20.130
their reads and writes
set, they don't need to

00:20:20.131 --> 00:20:21.850
do it for each individual call.

00:20:21.851 --> 00:20:23.770
But they lock the read
and write set

00:20:23.771 --> 00:20:26.670
not using the Middleware layer,

00:20:26.671 --> 00:20:29.520
but bypassing the Middleware.

00:20:29.521 --> 00:20:31.410
They lock the reads and writes

00:20:31.411 --> 00:20:34.100
sets into the Blockchain as well.

00:20:34.101 --> 00:20:35.800
The nice point about

00:20:35.801 --> 00:20:37.290
these calls is they don't

00:20:37.291 --> 00:20:38.835
need to be synchronous blocking,

00:20:38.836 --> 00:20:41.210
you can just send
them asynchronously.

00:20:41.211 --> 00:20:43.930
At the end of your Epoch when

00:20:43.931 --> 00:20:47.395
you execute it multiple
of the puts and gets,

00:20:47.396 --> 00:20:50.030
what you want to do is also
a verification procedure that

00:20:50.031 --> 00:20:52.780
looks into the question
if the write set was

00:20:52.781 --> 00:20:55.315
included that
the clients reported via

00:20:55.316 --> 00:20:57.360
their record calls is included in

00:20:57.361 --> 00:21:00.210
the write set that the key
value store was seen.

00:21:00.211 --> 00:21:03.355
This is also again a
non-blocking operation means we

00:21:03.356 --> 00:21:05.890
submit that as
a Blockchain transactions,

00:21:05.891 --> 00:21:08.510
so implemented as
a store procedure as

00:21:08.511 --> 00:21:10.430
a smart contract which checks

00:21:10.431 --> 00:21:13.155
if these two guarantees
are satisfied.

00:21:13.156 --> 00:21:15.105
Once it's done, we can just fetch

00:21:15.106 --> 00:21:16.780
the result if the
Epoch was correct,

00:21:16.781 --> 00:21:19.620
if all the operations
have been correct.

00:21:19.621 --> 00:21:21.885
In worst case, rollback

00:21:21.886 --> 00:21:24.005
in the transaction
history to a point

00:21:24.006 --> 00:21:31.330
where the last verified Epoch
was stored in the database.

00:21:31.331 --> 00:21:36.765
So, we can also recover
to valid checkpoints.

00:21:36.766 --> 00:21:39.955
Good. So there's much more,

00:21:39.956 --> 00:21:41.430
many more details that
you need to think

00:21:41.431 --> 00:21:43.100
about if you want to do

00:21:43.101 --> 00:21:45.350
verification with
Blockchain and there are

00:21:45.351 --> 00:21:47.670
more details I haven't been
including in the slides,

00:21:47.671 --> 00:21:50.800
but this is the general notion
of how we want to

00:21:50.801 --> 00:21:55.155
ensure that puts and gets
are executed correctly.

00:21:55.156 --> 00:21:57.455
So what are we up to next?

00:21:57.456 --> 00:21:59.110
So, as I said,

00:21:59.111 --> 00:22:01.390
it's a first step
that we implemented.

00:22:01.391 --> 00:22:03.615
There are many more optimizations

00:22:03.616 --> 00:22:04.895
that we haven't implemented.

00:22:04.896 --> 00:22:06.810
For example, caching
in the Middleware

00:22:06.811 --> 00:22:09.495
but still guaranteeing
verifiability.

00:22:09.496 --> 00:22:12.655
That's something we are
working on at the moment.

00:22:12.656 --> 00:22:16.025
Then for sure go in

00:22:16.026 --> 00:22:17.355
the route of having

00:22:17.356 --> 00:22:19.210
not just the put/get
interface but supporting

00:22:19.211 --> 00:22:23.500
full database transactions on

00:22:23.501 --> 00:22:26.495
top of our key value
store interface.

00:22:26.496 --> 00:22:28.480
So, in long-term,

00:22:28.481 --> 00:22:31.290
what we want to achieve
or how we think

00:22:31.291 --> 00:22:34.640
that our Middleware could be

00:22:34.641 --> 00:22:37.390
integrated into a data
management stack is also have

00:22:37.391 --> 00:22:40.990
a combination of a database
that is running on-premise,

00:22:40.991 --> 00:22:45.975
where you use the
Blockchain Database System

00:22:45.976 --> 00:22:48.080
to integrate something like

00:22:48.081 --> 00:22:51.070
a Shared column into
your local Database system.

00:22:51.071 --> 00:22:52.400
Meaning, you create a table,

00:22:52.401 --> 00:22:54.395
just mark your column
as "Shared",

00:22:54.396 --> 00:22:57.849
and by getting the guarantees of

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:00.720
verifiable transactions
and sharing on

00:23:00.721 --> 00:23:02.635
those columns of data
is automatically

00:23:02.636 --> 00:23:04.035
written to the Blockchain,

00:23:04.036 --> 00:23:06.010
and you can verify
for those columns

00:23:06.011 --> 00:23:08.405
if transactions on
those columns have been right.

00:23:08.406 --> 00:23:11.015
But this is something we haven't

00:23:11.016 --> 00:23:14.370
started thinking about in detail.

00:23:14.371 --> 00:23:17.200
With that, I'm at
the end of the talk,

00:23:17.201 --> 00:23:19.215
happy to take questions.

00:23:19.216 --> 00:23:24.100
I'm sure we have time for
questions at all. Yeah.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:32.695
&gt;&gt; So, you cited
a sigma paper saying that

00:23:32.696 --> 00:23:33.730
even if you set up a

00:23:33.731 --> 00:23:35.760
private environment
around Blockchain,

00:23:35.761 --> 00:23:37.650
the throughput is
very limited, right?

00:23:37.651 --> 00:23:38.035
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:38.036 --> 00:23:40.780
&gt;&gt; So, intuitively
I have hard time to

00:23:40.781 --> 00:23:42.010
understand why this case and I

00:23:42.011 --> 00:23:43.690
wonder if you can
share some insight.

00:23:43.691 --> 00:23:46.115
&gt;&gt; You mean why the transaction
rate of Blockchains.

00:23:46.116 --> 00:23:49.710
So, I haven't been going

00:23:49.711 --> 00:23:53.690
into the details of the
execution models of Blockchains.

00:23:53.691 --> 00:23:56.210
But for example,
public Blockchain use

00:23:56.211 --> 00:23:59.685
a consensus protocol which
is called "Proof of Work".

00:23:59.686 --> 00:24:02.785
Meaning, that one
was private, yeah.

00:24:02.786 --> 00:24:04.570
For them, as I said so,

00:24:04.571 --> 00:24:09.235
the complete transact
for private Blockchains,

00:24:09.236 --> 00:24:14.070
the problem is
that, the execution

00:24:14.071 --> 00:24:16.850
of all transactions
is fully serialized.

00:24:16.851 --> 00:24:18.950
Meaning one node takes a set

00:24:18.951 --> 00:24:22.080
of transactions, executes them,

00:24:22.081 --> 00:24:23.970
distributes the results to

00:24:23.971 --> 00:24:25.780
all the peers in
the Blockchain and

00:24:25.781 --> 00:24:27.480
they re-execute
the transactions to

00:24:27.481 --> 00:24:29.270
verify the outcome
of the transactions.

00:24:29.271 --> 00:24:33.390
Then, if different peers

00:24:33.391 --> 00:24:35.440
agrees or different peers or

00:24:35.441 --> 00:24:37.680
the majority agrees,
the block is appended.

00:24:37.681 --> 00:24:38.965
Which is inherently,

00:24:38.966 --> 00:24:42.770
so full replicated Database
System that doesn't allow

00:24:42.771 --> 00:24:45.520
any concurrency and
the results that you

00:24:45.521 --> 00:24:46.620
are seeing is and there are

00:24:46.621 --> 00:24:48.675
many more overheads
in Blockchains,

00:24:48.676 --> 00:24:51.020
which is not just
the execution but

00:24:51.021 --> 00:24:53.430
also verifying
the signatures for each and

00:24:53.431 --> 00:24:55.780
every transactions, etcetera.

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:05.470
Yeah. Mike.

00:25:05.471 --> 00:25:09.430
&gt;&gt; I'm just curious if
you could give us a feel.

00:25:09.431 --> 00:25:10.770
I know you didn't go
into the details of

00:25:10.771 --> 00:25:13.530
Blockchain Protocols
but is there something

00:25:13.531 --> 00:25:17.420
fundamentally
different with using

00:25:17.421 --> 00:25:18.910
Blockchain as the backend for

00:25:18.911 --> 00:25:20.820
the key value store
as opposed to if

00:25:20.821 --> 00:25:22.720
you were just
building a Federated

00:25:22.721 --> 00:25:23.900
Database over a bunch of

00:25:23.901 --> 00:25:26.220
slow Database Systems
where you would

00:25:26.221 --> 00:25:29.550
be faced with a lot of
the same challenges?

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:36.020
&gt;&gt; There are two answers from
my side to your question.

00:25:36.021 --> 00:25:38.960
The first one is,
why don't you use

00:25:38.961 --> 00:25:42.170
a Database System or

00:25:42.171 --> 00:25:43.870
replicate the Database System

00:25:43.871 --> 00:25:46.360
from the start for
those use cases.

00:25:46.361 --> 00:25:48.805
But that's a valid question.

00:25:48.806 --> 00:25:53.095
So, the answer is, for me,

00:25:53.096 --> 00:25:58.140
people are using
Blockchains nowadays and it

00:25:58.141 --> 00:26:00.510
has this legal notion that
data that is stored in

00:26:00.511 --> 00:26:03.010
a Blockchain can be used
as evidence for court.

00:26:03.011 --> 00:26:04.310
So, there are already

00:26:04.311 --> 00:26:07.000
some mechanisms in place that
you don't have a database.

00:26:07.001 --> 00:26:10.585
There are reasons why people
should use a Blockchain.

00:26:10.586 --> 00:26:14.570
For some use cases,

00:26:14.571 --> 00:26:15.750
it's not possible to start with

00:26:15.751 --> 00:26:18.605
a Database System as
a starting point.

00:26:18.606 --> 00:26:22.670
The second one is,
regarding the performance,

00:26:22.671 --> 00:26:24.230
I think that we saw already

00:26:24.231 --> 00:26:26.310
from the talk from
Microsoft in the morning,

00:26:26.311 --> 00:26:28.700
there's a lot going on

00:26:28.701 --> 00:26:32.290
in optimizing the performance

00:26:32.291 --> 00:26:34.920
of Blockchain Systems themselves.

00:26:34.921 --> 00:26:36.745
We just benefit from it.

00:26:36.746 --> 00:26:38.770
Whatever comes next
as a platform,

00:26:38.771 --> 00:26:41.750
we can plug it in as a backend
and use it and provide

00:26:41.751 --> 00:26:45.230
a verification guarantees on top.

00:26:45.231 --> 00:26:50.130
This is what, let's say
the value of what we provide is.

00:26:50.131 --> 00:26:52.490
So, you get the interface
of a typical Database,

00:26:52.491 --> 00:26:55.120
you can verify your transaction.

00:26:55.121 --> 00:26:58.050
So put get operations as if
it would be a Database but at

00:26:58.051 --> 00:27:01.150
the end, it's a Blockchain.

00:27:01.151 --> 00:27:03.620
So, we provide a stable interface

00:27:03.621 --> 00:27:05.410
you can program against it from

00:27:05.411 --> 00:27:07.470
your application and get

00:27:07.471 --> 00:27:11.150
the advantages from
your Blockchain Systems.

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:19.270
&gt;&gt; Assume we moved from
'Proof of Work" to "Proof of

00:27:19.271 --> 00:27:21.785
Stake" and assume every block

00:27:21.786 --> 00:27:24.110
can pack tens of transactions.

00:27:24.111 --> 00:27:26.815
Where would you imagine would
be the next bottleneck,

00:27:26.816 --> 00:27:28.780
in terms of improving

00:27:28.781 --> 00:27:31.255
the performance of Blockchain
transaction processing?

00:27:31.256 --> 00:27:34.450
&gt;&gt; We haven't been ourselves
not looking too much

00:27:34.451 --> 00:27:38.610
into where the bottlenecks
of current systems are.

00:27:38.611 --> 00:27:40.575
There two good papers

00:27:40.576 --> 00:27:42.050
that analyze actually

00:27:42.051 --> 00:27:43.630
the performance
bottlenecks and where

00:27:43.631 --> 00:27:46.545
are the limits of

00:27:46.546 --> 00:27:47.830
how far could we

00:27:47.831 --> 00:27:49.930
push the performance if
we tweak the parameters.

00:27:49.931 --> 00:27:53.370
There's one from a bunch of
authors from ETH, Cornell,

00:27:53.371 --> 00:27:55.970
et cetera, where they

00:27:55.971 --> 00:27:57.660
theoretically
analyzed where is the

00:27:57.661 --> 00:28:00.050
upper bound with
the current protocols.

00:28:00.051 --> 00:28:03.740
I think that's a nice read.

00:28:03.741 --> 00:28:06.910
Anyway, the second
one is Blockbench,

00:28:06.911 --> 00:28:10.250
where we're running
already against

00:28:10.251 --> 00:28:11.930
different systems with
different consensus

00:28:11.931 --> 00:28:14.690
protocol workloads.

00:28:14.691 --> 00:28:17.725
From my perspective, what

00:28:17.726 --> 00:28:20.590
I think Blockchain should do is,

00:28:20.591 --> 00:28:23.320
the computational
or the execution

00:28:23.321 --> 00:28:25.490
of transactions is
inherently inefficient.

00:28:25.491 --> 00:28:27.620
As I said, it's completely
sequentialized.

00:28:27.621 --> 00:28:28.860
There's no notion of concurrency,

00:28:28.861 --> 00:28:31.265
and I think Blockchains
can learn a lot from what

00:28:31.266 --> 00:28:33.740
databases have been
doing in concurrency.

00:28:33.741 --> 00:28:35.410
So, executing transactions as

00:28:35.411 --> 00:28:37.225
if they would be serialized.

00:28:37.226 --> 00:28:39.280
See, if they would be serial but

00:28:39.281 --> 00:28:41.450
having actually a concurrent
execution and if you

00:28:41.451 --> 00:28:43.920
can get some ideas of those into

00:28:43.921 --> 00:28:46.965
the next generation of
Blockchain Systems,

00:28:46.966 --> 00:28:48.130
it would increase the performance

00:28:48.131 --> 00:28:49.740
and reduce the bottleneck

00:28:49.741 --> 00:28:54.710
of what current Blockchains have.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:56.110
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:28:56.111 --> 00:28:58.775
I think we are running
slightly behind scheduled.

00:28:58.776 --> 00:29:00.315
If you have further questions,

00:29:00.316 --> 00:29:02.445
we might want to
do a short break.

00:29:02.446 --> 00:29:04.885
So, we'll thank the speaker. Yes.

00:29:04.886 --> 00:29:06.320
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:13.160
&gt;&gt; So, the next speaker in
this session is Srinath.

00:29:13.161 --> 00:29:15.830
So, Srinath as a researcher
at Microsoft Research

00:29:15.831 --> 00:29:19.000
at Redmond labs and
Srinath is an expert

00:29:19.001 --> 00:29:22.195
on having periodic systems

00:29:22.196 --> 00:29:24.665
around verifying of computation,

00:29:24.666 --> 00:29:26.280
around computation verification.

00:29:26.281 --> 00:29:27.930
In this talk, he's going to talk

00:29:27.931 --> 00:29:29.930
about a system called Spice,

00:29:29.931 --> 00:29:32.850
which is about
verifiable state machine

00:29:32.851 --> 00:29:36.090
and this work is going to
appear at OSDA this year.

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:43.370
&gt;&gt; Can you hear me?
Think it's working.

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:46.200
Okay, thanks.

00:29:46.201 --> 00:29:47.740
So this talk is about

00:29:47.741 --> 00:29:51.045
the powerful primitive card
Verifiable state machines.

00:29:51.046 --> 00:29:52.540
The system that implements this

00:29:52.541 --> 00:29:54.805
primitive is called Spice.

00:29:54.806 --> 00:29:57.040
This primitive as
many applications

00:29:57.041 --> 00:29:58.315
in the context stuff for

00:29:58.316 --> 00:30:00.020
trustworthy Cloud Services as

00:30:00.021 --> 00:30:02.330
well as de-centralized
Blockchains.

00:30:02.331 --> 00:30:05.755
I'll start by defining
what this primitive is.

00:30:05.756 --> 00:30:08.030
A Verfiiable state machine is a

00:30:08.031 --> 00:30:10.750
primitive in which there are
three types of entities;

00:30:10.751 --> 00:30:12.290
a set of clients,

00:30:12.291 --> 00:30:15.670
a statefull service,
and a set of verifiers.

00:30:15.671 --> 00:30:18.010
These clients issue
a st of request to

00:30:18.011 --> 00:30:20.765
the service and get back
a set of responses.

00:30:20.766 --> 00:30:23.265
Then the service
periodically publishes

00:30:23.266 --> 00:30:24.860
a trace which contains

00:30:24.861 --> 00:30:27.955
an entry for each request
response pair.

00:30:27.956 --> 00:30:31.480
Then each of these verifiers
run instead set of

00:30:31.481 --> 00:30:35.545
local checks and then
output, accept or reject.

00:30:35.546 --> 00:30:39.010
Then we call such a primitive as

00:30:39.011 --> 00:30:40.520
a Verifiable state machine if

00:30:40.521 --> 00:30:42.945
it satisfies these properties.

00:30:42.946 --> 00:30:45.920
First, if the service behaves,

00:30:45.921 --> 00:30:47.790
that is the execution
is equivalent to

00:30:47.791 --> 00:30:50.810
some serial execution of
the concurrent requests,

00:30:50.811 --> 00:30:53.780
then the verifier
is output accept.

00:30:53.781 --> 00:30:57.750
Second, if the service
misbehaves with

00:30:57.751 --> 00:30:59.330
execution of a request or

00:30:59.331 --> 00:31:01.525
with concurrency
control mechanisms,

00:31:01.526 --> 00:31:03.055
then the probability that

00:31:03.056 --> 00:31:07.160
a verifier outputs accept
should be less than epsilon,

00:31:07.161 --> 00:31:09.790
where epsilon is very small.

00:31:09.791 --> 00:31:13.070
Third, the trace itself
is zero knowledge,

00:31:13.071 --> 00:31:15.070
that is it does not
reveal anything about

00:31:15.071 --> 00:31:17.100
the requests or the responses

00:31:17.101 --> 00:31:18.830
or the internal
state of the service

00:31:18.831 --> 00:31:22.460
beyond the validity of
the correct request execution.

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:27.080
Finally, we require each entry
in this trace to be small,

00:31:27.081 --> 00:31:29.900
for example, a few 100 bytes.

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:36.545
It turns out, there's
a lot of prior work that

00:31:36.546 --> 00:31:37.810
implicitly implements

00:31:37.811 --> 00:31:40.565
this Verifiable State
machine abstraction.

00:31:40.566 --> 00:31:43.440
They do not use the state
machine formalism

00:31:43.441 --> 00:31:45.430
but it's not fundamental.

00:31:45.431 --> 00:31:49.580
The theory actually dates
back to early 90's.

00:31:49.581 --> 00:31:53.549
But this theory was too
expensive to be implemented.

00:31:53.550 --> 00:31:56.550
How does a lot of
work that reduces

00:31:56.551 --> 00:32:00.125
the cost of this theory by
all 20 orders of magnitude?

00:32:00.126 --> 00:32:03.215
These latter systems, supplied

00:32:03.216 --> 00:32:05.390
support for stateful computations

00:32:05.391 --> 00:32:07.400
in those earlier systems.

00:32:07.401 --> 00:32:10.655
For example, they support
storage interfaces such as

00:32:10.656 --> 00:32:12.800
key-value stores and
even a limited form

00:32:12.801 --> 00:32:15.290
of SQL Databases.

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:19.260
Despite this massive progress,

00:32:19.261 --> 00:32:22.530
all of these systems have
two key limitations.

00:32:22.531 --> 00:32:24.730
First, the storage operations

00:32:24.731 --> 00:32:26.640
in these systems
are very expensive.

00:32:26.641 --> 00:32:29.600
For example, it would
take tens of seconds or

00:32:29.601 --> 00:32:33.660
even several minutes for
each storage operation.

00:32:33.661 --> 00:32:36.850
Second limitation is
that they only support

00:32:36.851 --> 00:32:37.990
a sequential model of

00:32:37.991 --> 00:32:41.360
execution for expressing
these services.

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:45.600
As a result of
these two limitations,

00:32:45.601 --> 00:32:47.130
prior work can only support

00:32:47.131 --> 00:32:51.560
very limited throughput even
for very simple services.

00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:55.720
Our system, which is called

00:32:55.721 --> 00:32:58.425
Spice addresses
these limitations.

00:32:58.426 --> 00:33:02.150
First, it features a new
storage primitive that is

00:33:02.151 --> 00:33:03.510
two orders of magnitude more

00:33:03.511 --> 00:33:06.395
efficient than
prior instantiations.

00:33:06.396 --> 00:33:09.020
Second, it supports
a concurrent model of

00:33:09.021 --> 00:33:12.145
execution for
expressing services.

00:33:12.146 --> 00:33:15.830
As a result of these two
new techniques,

00:33:15.831 --> 00:33:18.150
Spice can support thousands
of transactions per

00:33:18.151 --> 00:33:21.960
second for many realistic
applications.

00:33:23.380 --> 00:33:25.635
In the rest of
this talk, I'm going

00:33:25.636 --> 00:33:28.725
to focus on three things.

00:33:28.726 --> 00:33:31.080
First, I'll discuss
a few applications that

00:33:31.081 --> 00:33:33.780
can be built with
verifiable state machines.

00:33:33.781 --> 00:33:36.390
Second, I'll present
some background on

00:33:36.391 --> 00:33:39.660
prior work and
a quick overview of Spice.

00:33:39.661 --> 00:33:43.300
Finally, I'll present
some experimental results.

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:47.515
At a very high level,

00:33:47.516 --> 00:33:48.620
we are interested in

00:33:48.621 --> 00:33:52.215
this primitive verifiable state
machines for two reasons.

00:33:52.216 --> 00:33:55.490
First, it enables us to
build cloud services in

00:33:55.491 --> 00:33:59.265
which we do not have to trust
the Cloud infrastructure.

00:33:59.266 --> 00:34:03.370
Second, it also enables private
and efficient Blockchains

00:34:03.371 --> 00:34:05.250
both in the permission
membership model

00:34:05.251 --> 00:34:07.530
and permission-less
membership model.

00:34:07.531 --> 00:34:11.365
I'll start with the first
application scenario.

00:34:11.366 --> 00:34:14.650
The first application is
a Cloud-hosted ledger

00:34:14.651 --> 00:34:16.845
inspired by Sequence.

00:34:16.846 --> 00:34:19.285
Here, the service maintains

00:34:19.286 --> 00:34:22.880
balances of assets owned
by different clients.

00:34:22.881 --> 00:34:26.220
It exposes three types
of requests.

00:34:26.221 --> 00:34:29.360
First, the Issue operation
enables an issuer which is

00:34:29.361 --> 00:34:31.490
a special entity in the system

00:34:31.491 --> 00:34:34.515
to issue some assets to a client.

00:34:34.516 --> 00:34:37.760
The second operation is
a Transfer that allows

00:34:37.761 --> 00:34:41.405
one client to transfer an asset
to a different client.

00:34:41.406 --> 00:34:43.910
Finally, the last operation
which is called

00:34:43.911 --> 00:34:45.490
Retire allows a client to

00:34:45.491 --> 00:34:48.320
take an asset out of the system.

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:52.590
So in the status quo,
if somebody wants to

00:34:52.591 --> 00:34:55.160
verify the correct execution
of this service,

00:34:55.161 --> 00:34:57.820
they have to get a complete
trace of all the requests,

00:34:57.821 --> 00:35:01.100
responses, and the internal
state of the service.

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:04.510
An auditor can verify

00:35:04.511 --> 00:35:06.020
the correct execution
of the service

00:35:06.021 --> 00:35:09.610
without access to any of the
requests or the responses.

00:35:09.611 --> 00:35:11.370
It does not have to trust

00:35:11.371 --> 00:35:15.340
the infrastructure on which
the services is running.

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:22.000
The second application
is in the context of

00:35:22.001 --> 00:35:25.430
four decentralized
Blockchain such as Ethereum.

00:35:25.431 --> 00:35:28.110
On Ethereum, a smart contract is

00:35:28.111 --> 00:35:29.700
essentially a state machine

00:35:29.701 --> 00:35:31.595
where two or more counter-parties

00:35:31.596 --> 00:35:38.465
can issue transactions to
create state transitions.

00:35:38.466 --> 00:35:40.565
So in the status quo,

00:35:40.566 --> 00:35:41.800
all the transactions are

00:35:41.801 --> 00:35:43.525
actually stored on
the Blockchain.

00:35:43.526 --> 00:35:46.095
So this provides
no confidentiality guarantees.

00:35:46.096 --> 00:35:49.090
Anyone in the world can
inspect the internal state of

00:35:49.091 --> 00:35:50.800
the contract or also look at

00:35:50.801 --> 00:35:54.485
all the state transitions
a contract has gone through.

00:35:54.486 --> 00:35:57.440
Second, every request from

00:35:57.441 --> 00:35:59.550
the app must be processed
by the Blockchain.

00:35:59.551 --> 00:36:03.265
So this limits the
application level throughput

00:36:03.266 --> 00:36:05.280
that you can get by storing

00:36:05.281 --> 00:36:07.560
a smart contract on
a public Blockchain.

00:36:07.561 --> 00:36:13.060
But as with verifiable
state machine,

00:36:13.061 --> 00:36:14.740
you don't actually
have to execute

00:36:14.741 --> 00:36:17.150
those smart contracts
on the Blockchain.

00:36:17.151 --> 00:36:20.630
You can execute them outside
the Blockchain and then

00:36:20.631 --> 00:36:22.575
process only a succinct

00:36:22.576 --> 00:36:25.235
zero-knowledge trace
on the Blockchain.

00:36:25.236 --> 00:36:29.120
Because this succinct trace
does not reveal anything

00:36:29.121 --> 00:36:30.325
about the internal state

00:36:30.326 --> 00:36:32.885
of the requests or the responses,

00:36:32.886 --> 00:36:37.500
it provides very strong
confidentiality guarantees.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:42.170
Because Ethereum will only
process a succinct trace,

00:36:42.171 --> 00:36:44.240
you can actually support
very high throughput for

00:36:44.241 --> 00:36:45.855
your applications which is

00:36:45.856 --> 00:36:49.540
independent of the throughput
supported by Ethereum.

00:36:53.290 --> 00:36:56.530
So I hope that I convinced you

00:36:56.531 --> 00:36:59.550
that verifiable state
machines are actually useful.

00:36:59.551 --> 00:37:01.190
So in the next part of this talk,

00:37:01.191 --> 00:37:04.330
I'm going to provide some
background on a prior system

00:37:04.331 --> 00:37:08.890
called Pantry before I
provide an overview of Spice.

00:37:10.760 --> 00:37:15.260
So, Pantry itself extends
to prior systems called

00:37:15.261 --> 00:37:19.385
Zaatar and Pinocchio to
support a notion of state.

00:37:19.386 --> 00:37:21.840
All these three systems
are composed

00:37:21.841 --> 00:37:24.060
of a front-end and a back-end.

00:37:24.061 --> 00:37:26.445
This front-end
translates a C program

00:37:26.446 --> 00:37:28.725
into a set of
algebraic constraints.

00:37:28.726 --> 00:37:30.360
Then the back-end implements

00:37:30.361 --> 00:37:31.890
an argument protocol to prove

00:37:31.891 --> 00:37:34.830
the correct execution
of the C program.

00:37:34.970 --> 00:37:37.370
I'm not going to go
into the details of

00:37:37.371 --> 00:37:39.235
how these components work,

00:37:39.236 --> 00:37:40.670
but I'll just observe that

00:37:40.671 --> 00:37:42.470
both Zaatar and Pinocchio support

00:37:42.471 --> 00:37:45.900
a large subset of C. This,

00:37:45.901 --> 00:37:47.820
in turn, enables verifying

00:37:47.821 --> 00:37:51.000
the execution of
stateless programs.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:54.840
Then Pantry supports
state while working in

00:37:54.841 --> 00:37:57.295
the same stateless
computation model

00:37:57.296 --> 00:38:00.719
by leveraging
cryptographic hashes.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:05.170
I'm going to briefly tell
you how this is done.

00:38:05.210 --> 00:38:09.480
So the key idea in Pantry
is to name data blocks

00:38:09.481 --> 00:38:13.770
using a short cryptographic
digest of those data blocks.

00:38:13.771 --> 00:38:15.980
Here's a very small
C program that

00:38:15.981 --> 00:38:18.720
illustrates the key
idea in Pantry.

00:38:18.721 --> 00:38:22.030
It takes as input
a digest and then

00:38:22.031 --> 00:38:26.035
the prover of the service
can actually supply state.

00:38:26.036 --> 00:38:28.240
Of course, because
the service is entrusted,

00:38:28.241 --> 00:38:31.475
it can support a supply
any state to this program.

00:38:31.476 --> 00:38:34.080
But there is an assert
statement that checks if

00:38:34.081 --> 00:38:36.440
the digest supplied
by the Verifier

00:38:36.441 --> 00:38:38.870
equals the cryptographic hash

00:38:38.871 --> 00:38:41.705
of the block supplied
by the service.

00:38:41.706 --> 00:38:45.560
If the service supplies
the correct state,

00:38:45.561 --> 00:38:49.290
just check passes;
otherwise, this check fails.

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:53.260
By using this idea,

00:38:53.261 --> 00:38:55.840
Pantry builds a key-value store.

00:38:55.841 --> 00:38:58.275
The core idea is to treat

00:38:58.276 --> 00:39:01.910
hashes of data as
pointers to such data.

00:39:01.911 --> 00:39:04.320
Then you can build
a tree and which in

00:39:04.321 --> 00:39:08.080
turn can be used to
build a key value store.

00:39:09.140 --> 00:39:11.230
But the fundamental problem in

00:39:11.231 --> 00:39:12.630
this approach is that cost of

00:39:12.631 --> 00:39:14.060
a key-value store operation is

00:39:14.061 --> 00:39:16.655
logarithmic in
the size of the state.

00:39:16.656 --> 00:39:18.700
Completely, this means it

00:39:18.701 --> 00:39:20.485
takes several minutes of CPU time

00:39:20.486 --> 00:39:24.890
even with the million
key-value pairs in your state.

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:30.285
Spice addresses
these performance problems.

00:39:30.286 --> 00:39:33.030
The core idea in Spice is we use

00:39:33.031 --> 00:39:36.890
a set data structure instead
of a tree data structure.

00:39:36.891 --> 00:39:40.085
We then map key-value
store operations

00:39:40.086 --> 00:39:42.910
to operations on a set.

00:39:42.911 --> 00:39:47.520
As a result, the cost of
a storage operation is

00:39:47.521 --> 00:39:49.345
constant time instead of

00:39:49.346 --> 00:39:52.540
logarithmic on
an amortized basis.

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:57.050
However, if we instantiate
this idea naively,

00:39:57.051 --> 00:39:58.640
it's actually going to be more

00:39:58.641 --> 00:40:00.945
expensive than
the tree-based approach,

00:40:00.946 --> 00:40:03.790
constraints involved
and in the case

00:40:03.791 --> 00:40:07.960
of many data sizes that
we are interested in.

00:40:08.140 --> 00:40:12.360
However, Spice
solves this problem.

00:40:12.361 --> 00:40:16.290
It does this by using
an efficient instantiation of

00:40:16.291 --> 00:40:18.390
the set-based data structure

00:40:18.391 --> 00:40:21.240
using elliptic
curve cryptography.

00:40:21.241 --> 00:40:24.690
Spice also includes
new techniques to execute

00:40:24.691 --> 00:40:26.240
these state transitions or

00:40:26.241 --> 00:40:29.560
transactions inexpensively
in this model.

00:40:29.561 --> 00:40:31.700
I'm not going to go
into the details

00:40:31.701 --> 00:40:33.260
of how all of this is done,

00:40:33.261 --> 00:40:35.650
but I'll just present
a few implementation details

00:40:35.651 --> 00:40:38.240
and how the system looks.

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:42.760
We implement Spice
on top of Pantry.

00:40:42.761 --> 00:40:45.340
We also wrote three
applications using Spice,

00:40:45.341 --> 00:40:47.350
but Spice includes
a toolchain using,

00:40:47.351 --> 00:40:50.470
but you can build
many more applications.

00:40:50.471 --> 00:40:54.330
The toolchain takes as input
a C program which expresses

00:40:54.331 --> 00:40:57.950
a request handler and then
it outputs two executables,

00:40:57.951 --> 00:41:01.040
one for the service and
one for the verifier.

00:41:01.041 --> 00:41:03.950
This request handler can include

00:41:03.951 --> 00:41:08.515
arbitrary C or can also call
into Spice's storage APIs.

00:41:08.516 --> 00:41:12.350
The storage primitive
itself exposes many APIs.

00:41:12.351 --> 00:41:14.610
First it provides you operations

00:41:14.611 --> 00:41:17.610
and key-value stores
such as get and put.

00:41:17.611 --> 00:41:21.995
Second, it allows
mutual exclusion

00:41:21.996 --> 00:41:24.070
of APIs such as lock and unlock,

00:41:24.071 --> 00:41:26.290
so you can lock a key, perform

00:41:26.291 --> 00:41:28.220
an update, and then unlock.

00:41:28.221 --> 00:41:31.775
It also provides a restricted
form of transactions.

00:41:31.776 --> 00:41:35.150
So, you can call begin
transactions on a set of keys,

00:41:35.151 --> 00:41:38.110
it returns the current state
of those keys.

00:41:38.111 --> 00:41:40.680
Then you can do arbitrary
computation and then you can

00:41:40.681 --> 00:41:42.460
call end transactions with

00:41:42.461 --> 00:41:44.310
the values that you
want to write back.

00:41:44.311 --> 00:41:47.315
All of this update
happens atomically.

00:41:47.316 --> 00:41:53.000
In the next part of this talk,

00:41:53.001 --> 00:41:55.870
I'm going to present
some experimental results.

00:41:55.871 --> 00:41:58.445
There are two questions
that I'm interested in.

00:41:58.446 --> 00:41:59.750
First, how does Spice

00:41:59.751 --> 00:42:02.500
compare with prior
state-of-the-art?

00:42:02.501 --> 00:42:04.910
Second, what is the
end-to-end performance

00:42:04.911 --> 00:42:07.440
of applications built with Spice?

00:42:07.441 --> 00:42:09.780
To answer these questions,
we run a set of

00:42:09.781 --> 00:42:12.560
experiments on Azure Cluster.

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:16.305
So for the first question,
we measure the throughput of

00:42:16.306 --> 00:42:17.890
key-value store operations under

00:42:17.891 --> 00:42:21.340
Spice as well as a set
of baseline systems.

00:42:21.341 --> 00:42:23.520
We preload the
key-value store with

00:42:23.521 --> 00:42:26.660
a million key-value
pairs and then

00:42:26.661 --> 00:42:28.105
we measure the throughput of

00:42:28.106 --> 00:42:32.430
the system for get
input operations separately.

00:42:32.431 --> 00:42:35.810
As against C, due to
new techniques in Spice,

00:42:35.811 --> 00:42:37.910
Spice can achieve
up to four orders of

00:42:37.911 --> 00:42:41.670
magnitude speed up compared
to the baseline systems.

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:45.920
So to answer the second question,

00:42:45.921 --> 00:42:48.710
we implemented three
applications and this graph

00:42:48.711 --> 00:42:50.800
depicts end-to-end
performance with

00:42:50.801 --> 00:42:53.200
varying number of CPU cores.

00:42:53.201 --> 00:42:56.030
In the x-axis, you
can see we have

00:42:56.031 --> 00:42:58.950
depicted various requests
types such as issue,

00:42:58.951 --> 00:43:00.615
transfer and retire,

00:43:00.616 --> 00:43:03.475
that are supported by
these applications.

00:43:03.476 --> 00:43:06.830
As we can see, Spice achieves
a near linear speedup

00:43:06.831 --> 00:43:11.090
and throughput as we
provide more CPU cores.

00:43:11.091 --> 00:43:13.900
I did not depict
the verifier's throughput,

00:43:13.901 --> 00:43:15.530
but it's much more than

00:43:15.531 --> 00:43:17.510
the prover's throughput of
the services throughput which

00:43:17.511 --> 00:43:20.060
is 15 million proof verifications

00:43:20.061 --> 00:43:22.550
per second on the same cluster.

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:27.890
So to summarize this talk,

00:43:27.891 --> 00:43:30.940
we believe verifiable state
machines is a key tool in

00:43:30.941 --> 00:43:33.065
the context of
both Cloud computing

00:43:33.066 --> 00:43:35.200
and decentralized Blockchains.

00:43:35.201 --> 00:43:38.280
Spice represents
a substantial progress towards

00:43:38.281 --> 00:43:41.390
efficiently implementing
verifiable state machines.

00:43:41.391 --> 00:43:43.545
Finally, we are excited about

00:43:43.546 --> 00:43:47.185
the many possibilities
that this work points to.

00:43:47.186 --> 00:43:51.490
With that, I can take questions.

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:11.740
Someone has a question.

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:17.230
I'm sorry, I kind of lost

00:44:17.231 --> 00:44:19.630
the connection between
spice and the Blockchain.

00:44:19.631 --> 00:44:21.280
So, where exactly,

00:44:21.281 --> 00:44:23.610
are you using spices'
technology to implement

00:44:23.611 --> 00:44:25.350
Blockchains or is
it a front end to

00:44:25.351 --> 00:44:27.650
Blockchains because you're
using a code generator?

00:44:27.651 --> 00:44:29.955
So, I don't know exactly
where the connection is.

00:44:29.956 --> 00:44:34.820
&gt;&gt; There are two applications
that I guess I can go back.

00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:44.650
Here is scenario that I presented

00:44:44.651 --> 00:44:46.055
in the context of Blockchain.

00:44:46.056 --> 00:44:47.540
So history of running

00:44:47.541 --> 00:44:49.650
the entire smart contract
on the Blockchain,

00:44:49.651 --> 00:44:52.030
and that executing the contract

00:44:52.031 --> 00:44:54.620
on every node in
the Blockchain system,

00:44:54.621 --> 00:44:59.880
you could run it once and
then you could just process

00:44:59.881 --> 00:45:02.945
that succinct zero knowledge
trace on the Blockchain.

00:45:02.946 --> 00:45:06.020
So, the entire Blockchain
network would just

00:45:06.021 --> 00:45:09.765
verify that there was
a valid state transition,

00:45:09.766 --> 00:45:11.520
and it does not
have to re-execute

00:45:11.521 --> 00:45:13.360
any transactions and
they also don't need to

00:45:13.361 --> 00:45:16.260
know what the contract

00:45:16.261 --> 00:45:19.370
was or what this
contract was doing.

00:45:19.371 --> 00:45:22.265
So it provides confidentiality
and also scalability.

00:45:22.266 --> 00:45:25.190
&gt;&gt; So, you are basing like
a front end to the Blockchain?

00:45:25.191 --> 00:45:26.070
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:45:26.071 --> 00:45:27.320
&gt;&gt; So, you don't need to use

00:45:27.321 --> 00:45:29.395
a Blockchain in the back and
you could put in a database.

00:45:29.396 --> 00:45:30.350
&gt;&gt; Sure. Yeah.

00:45:30.351 --> 00:45:33.135
&gt;&gt; If I did it, the Blockchain
is actually not relevant.

00:45:33.136 --> 00:45:35.995
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, here,

00:45:35.996 --> 00:45:37.770
only the verifier is
running the Blockchain but

00:45:37.771 --> 00:45:39.495
you could run
that verifier anywhere.

00:45:39.496 --> 00:45:39.785
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:45:39.786 --> 00:45:41.860
&gt;&gt; Even the service itself
could be running outside

00:45:41.861 --> 00:45:44.110
the Blockchain using
traditional databases.

00:45:44.111 --> 00:45:45.950
&gt;&gt; All right. Okay. Thank you.

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:57.730
&gt;&gt; The trace includes all the
hashes of the state. Yes.

00:45:57.731 --> 00:45:59.235
&gt;&gt; Can you return to the slide

00:45:59.236 --> 00:46:01.475
that gave the big picture of

00:46:01.476 --> 00:46:04.280
requests going into
the Blockchain and

00:46:04.281 --> 00:46:07.850
the trace going to the verifies?

00:46:11.620 --> 00:46:13.300
&gt;&gt; Is it this slide?

00:46:13.301 --> 00:46:15.180
&gt;&gt; Yes. Could you say

00:46:15.181 --> 00:46:17.390
what information the verifiers

00:46:17.391 --> 00:46:20.335
have access to in order
to make the decision?

00:46:20.336 --> 00:46:23.555
This picture depicts only
an append-only trace.

00:46:23.556 --> 00:46:25.560
Are they aware of
other information?

00:46:25.561 --> 00:46:28.700
&gt;&gt; Yes, they do know the
specification of the service.

00:46:28.701 --> 00:46:32.470
For example, what means for
taking a state transition,

00:46:32.471 --> 00:46:33.770
so they know the code that

00:46:33.771 --> 00:46:37.130
represents the state transition.

00:46:37.131 --> 00:46:40.010
They also know some encoding

00:46:40.011 --> 00:46:41.850
of the requests and
the encoding of the responses.

00:46:41.851 --> 00:46:43.220
So, this trace has an entry

00:46:43.221 --> 00:46:45.165
for each request response pair.

00:46:45.166 --> 00:46:47.080
&gt;&gt; So the verifiers are

00:46:47.081 --> 00:46:49.845
aware of something
about the requests.

00:46:49.846 --> 00:46:50.830
&gt;&gt; They don't know

00:46:50.831 --> 00:46:53.160
the actual plaintext contents
of the request,

00:46:53.161 --> 00:46:55.250
but they have
a succinct encoding which

00:46:55.251 --> 00:46:58.700
is a commitment to
those requests.

00:46:58.701 --> 00:47:00.580
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:47:09.890 --> 00:47:12.270
&gt;&gt; Quick question while
we're on this slide.

00:47:12.271 --> 00:47:16.825
So, you defined soundness
in terms of some epsilon.

00:47:16.826 --> 00:47:18.835
So what should I think
of epsilon as being

00:47:18.836 --> 00:47:20.030
like the Internet is very large,

00:47:20.031 --> 00:47:21.195
and there are many requests?

00:47:21.196 --> 00:47:21.650
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:47:21.651 --> 00:47:23.110
&gt;&gt; So, how often in
practice would you

00:47:23.111 --> 00:47:25.425
expect soundness to be violated??

00:47:25.426 --> 00:47:29.380
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, this epsilon
is one and two power 128,

00:47:29.381 --> 00:47:31.619
so you can think of it
as 128 bit security.

00:47:31.620 --> 00:47:35.625
So, it should be very small
like you should absolutely

00:47:35.626 --> 00:47:37.690
not see verified accepting

00:47:37.691 --> 00:47:40.170
some incorrect
statement as correct,

00:47:40.171 --> 00:47:44.720
except with that one
and two power 128,

00:47:44.721 --> 00:47:47.770
which is the security of
many crypto primitives that

00:47:47.771 --> 00:47:59.340
we use.

00:47:59.341 --> 00:48:00.580
Thank you.

