00:52:25.696 --> 00:52:28.050
So, this motivating example is

00:52:28.051 --> 00:52:30.464
in the area of fraud detection.

00:52:30.465 --> 00:52:32.595
So for example, for banks,

00:52:32.596 --> 00:52:34.620
they need to do
fraud detection to

00:52:34.621 --> 00:52:36.855
figure out whether they
should give someone

00:52:36.856 --> 00:52:43.455
along or with that suspicious
and malicious activities.

00:52:43.456 --> 00:52:47.370
So, typically, high
works is that each bank

00:52:47.371 --> 00:52:52.425
using its information can
change its own fraud detector,

00:52:52.426 --> 00:52:56.235
and because the data is

00:52:56.236 --> 00:52:58.410
sensitive and usually
it is difficult to

00:52:58.411 --> 00:53:01.260
find different banks to
collaborate together.

00:53:01.261 --> 00:53:04.590
However, as we all know,

00:53:04.591 --> 00:53:09.870
the effectiveness of
these tab of models,

00:53:09.871 --> 00:53:12.150
and it really depends on

00:53:12.151 --> 00:53:15.630
the amount of data that
the model is trained on or

00:53:15.631 --> 00:53:23.090
the broad views that
the model actually had seen.

00:53:23.091 --> 00:53:27.020
Hence, it would have been
really nice if we can

00:53:27.021 --> 00:53:28.760
develop a technology that

00:53:28.761 --> 00:53:31.190
allows these different banks
to collaborate together,

00:53:31.191 --> 00:53:35.015
and using the data from
each of the banks together

00:53:35.016 --> 00:53:40.060
to develop a better machinery
model for fraud detector.

00:53:42.050 --> 00:53:44.910
Unfortunately, today, we don't

00:53:44.911 --> 00:53:47.040
have a technology to enable

00:53:47.041 --> 00:53:51.705
the banks to do this because
of privacy concerns,

00:53:51.706 --> 00:53:55.720
regulatory risks, and
the misaligned incentives.

00:53:56.610 --> 00:53:58.945
&gt;&gt; So, this is an example where

00:53:58.946 --> 00:54:01.285
actually Blockchain can
help and in particular,

00:54:01.286 --> 00:54:03.940
a new type of
a Blockchain technology

00:54:03.941 --> 00:54:07.210
such as the ones that
we're developing can help.

00:54:07.211 --> 00:54:11.545
We can actually develop
smart contract,

00:54:11.546 --> 00:54:13.840
in this case, a fraud
detector smart contract,

00:54:13.841 --> 00:54:16.760
that runs on top
of the Blockchain.

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:20.935
In this case,
the different banks,

00:54:20.936 --> 00:54:22.750
and each of them can
actually contribute

00:54:22.751 --> 00:54:25.150
data to this smart contracts,

00:54:25.151 --> 00:54:27.970
and that together using

00:54:27.971 --> 00:54:30.025
the data from
these different sources,

00:54:30.026 --> 00:54:32.560
the fraud detector
smart contract can

00:54:32.561 --> 00:54:36.410
change fraud detection model.

00:54:37.710 --> 00:54:41.185
Hopefully, this
fraud detection model that

00:54:41.186 --> 00:54:44.350
change using data from
different banks can

00:54:44.351 --> 00:54:47.080
be much more effective
and can really help

00:54:47.081 --> 00:54:51.080
each bank to deal with
the fraud much better.

00:54:51.480 --> 00:54:56.035
However, in order to enable
an application like this,

00:54:56.036 --> 00:55:00.700
we need to solve a number
of challenges and issues.

00:55:00.701 --> 00:55:04.420
So, first, in
a typical Blockchain,

00:55:04.421 --> 00:55:05.560
actually, our data in

00:55:05.561 --> 00:55:08.635
compute on the
Blockchain is public.

00:55:08.636 --> 00:55:10.915
In this case, we are dealing with

00:55:10.916 --> 00:55:16.715
very sensitive data, essentially
customers' transactions.

00:55:16.716 --> 00:55:20.775
So, we need to handle
the challenge of

00:55:20.776 --> 00:55:25.475
having sensitive data on
the Blockchain worker notes,

00:55:25.476 --> 00:55:29.905
essentially protecting
the computation process of

00:55:29.906 --> 00:55:32.214
this much contract execution

00:55:32.215 --> 00:55:34.735
from leaking
sensitive information.

00:55:34.736 --> 00:55:38.005
Secondly, the computer
fraud detection model

00:55:38.006 --> 00:55:41.230
itself can potentially leak
sensitive information about

00:55:41.231 --> 00:55:45.145
the inputs because the
machine in the model

00:55:45.146 --> 00:55:50.590
here that is changed from
sensitive inputs to start with,

00:55:50.591 --> 00:55:53.350
and I'll show a little bit later

00:55:53.351 --> 00:55:55.870
that our work has shown

00:55:55.871 --> 00:55:58.795
that even when you
turn machinery model,

00:55:58.796 --> 00:56:02.830
I feel I'm now careful with
the privacy protection than

00:56:02.831 --> 00:56:05.080
this two-dimensional boundary
itself can actually

00:56:05.081 --> 00:56:08.750
leak sensitive information
about the inputs.

00:56:08.820 --> 00:56:13.390
Finally, to this Blockchain has

00:56:13.391 --> 00:56:15.880
a huge scalability issues and to

00:56:15.881 --> 00:56:19.420
this Blockchain has poor
performance and very high costs.

00:56:19.421 --> 00:56:23.920
So again, in order to enable
an application like this,

00:56:23.921 --> 00:56:28.750
we really need to be able to
scale up the Blockchain and

00:56:28.751 --> 00:56:34.735
scalability to enable a real-world
applications like this,

00:56:34.736 --> 00:56:37.950
and ideally at
the class scale event.

00:56:37.951 --> 00:56:39.730
&gt;&gt; Question. What about

00:56:39.731 --> 00:56:44.020
the smart contract that
the fraud detection model?

00:56:44.021 --> 00:56:45.450
Isn't that private?

00:56:45.451 --> 00:56:47.590
Isn't that important too?

00:56:47.591 --> 00:56:49.465
&gt;&gt; So, that's why I
will talk about this.

00:56:49.466 --> 00:56:51.580
Yes. So, we're actually
going to develop

00:56:51.581 --> 00:56:53.200
a privacy preserving
smart contracts

00:56:53.201 --> 00:56:55.700
that can help save others.

00:56:57.000 --> 00:57:00.550
So, in order to enable
application like this

00:57:00.551 --> 00:57:03.685
and address the challenges
that I just mentioned,

00:57:03.686 --> 00:57:05.845
an Oasis where developing

00:57:05.846 --> 00:57:10.015
a new Blockchain technology
called privacy,

00:57:10.016 --> 00:57:12.835
one of the key about
Blockchain platform

00:57:12.836 --> 00:57:16.810
is Privacy-preserving
Smart Contracts at Scale.

00:57:16.811 --> 00:57:20.500
So first, one of the key

00:57:20.501 --> 00:57:22.315
primitive of our platform is

00:57:22.316 --> 00:57:24.310
a Privacy-preserving
Smart Contracts.

00:57:24.311 --> 00:57:28.045
The premise of Privacy-preserving
Smart Contracts

00:57:28.046 --> 00:57:33.295
satisfies a number of unique
properties and capabilities.

00:57:33.296 --> 00:57:35.559
It enables automatic enforcement

00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:37.645
of codified privacy requirements.

00:57:37.646 --> 00:57:39.355
So, the privacy requirements here

00:57:39.356 --> 00:57:43.760
as coded into
the smart contracts.

00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:46.720
We can enforce this
without relying on

00:57:46.721 --> 00:57:49.210
any central party
and it's designed to

00:57:49.211 --> 00:57:50.920
scale to real-world applications

00:57:50.921 --> 00:57:54.340
including heavy workloads
such as machine learning.

00:57:54.341 --> 00:57:58.150
We design the technology to make

00:57:58.151 --> 00:58:01.510
it easy to use and
to make it much

00:58:01.511 --> 00:58:03.940
easier for Developers to build

00:58:03.941 --> 00:58:06.700
the privacy-preserving
applications without

00:58:06.701 --> 00:58:09.740
needing to be a privacy experts.

00:58:10.290 --> 00:58:12.790
In order to enable

00:58:12.791 --> 00:58:15.445
this Privacy-preserving
Smart Contract at Scale,

00:58:15.446 --> 00:58:17.845
we developed a number of

00:58:17.846 --> 00:58:21.220
different technologies
and combined

00:58:21.221 --> 00:58:25.910
together to build
our Oasis Blockchain platform.

00:58:25.950 --> 00:58:29.275
The Oasis Blockchain platform
essentially has,

00:58:29.276 --> 00:58:30.910
you can do to it's
two main parts,

00:58:30.911 --> 00:58:35.050
there's the platform
component and there is

00:58:35.051 --> 00:58:38.620
the application components
where the smart contracts is

00:58:38.621 --> 00:58:42.865
the abstraction at
the application level.

00:58:42.866 --> 00:58:48.025
So, at the platform
level, so first, we build

00:58:48.026 --> 00:58:51.430
confidentiality-preserving
smart contract execution to

00:58:51.431 --> 00:58:54.715
protect the smart contract
execution process

00:58:54.716 --> 00:58:58.495
from leaking sensitive
information about the inputs.

00:58:58.496 --> 00:59:03.595
At the application level
within the smart contract,

00:59:03.596 --> 00:59:08.260
we provide capabilities for
privacy-preserving analytics

00:59:08.261 --> 00:59:10.000
and machine learning to make it

00:59:10.001 --> 00:59:12.880
easy for Developers
to do analytics and

00:59:12.881 --> 00:59:16.540
machine learning without
needing to worry about privacy,

00:59:16.541 --> 00:59:21.260
and to enforce the desire
to privacy policies.

00:59:22.950 --> 00:59:25.705
At the platform level,

00:59:25.706 --> 00:59:28.915
we also developed
a new architecture

00:59:28.916 --> 00:59:30.820
for the Blockchain to enable

00:59:30.821 --> 00:59:34.135
scalable smart contract
execution in particular

00:59:34.136 --> 00:59:38.950
for scalability for complex
smart contract execution.

00:59:38.951 --> 00:59:41.605
So, now, let me talk about

00:59:41.606 --> 00:59:47.230
each component
technology separately.

00:59:47.231 --> 00:59:49.480
So, first, let me talk about

00:59:49.481 --> 00:59:53.570
the confidentiality-preserving
smart contract execution.

00:59:54.150 --> 00:59:57.175
So typically, again,
as I mentioned,

00:59:57.176 --> 00:59:59.185
so most Blockchain today,

00:59:59.186 --> 01:00:02.890
they data and compute our
public on the Blockchain,

01:00:02.891 --> 01:00:06.520
hence they can only have
very limited used cases.

01:00:06.521 --> 01:00:09.025
So, in this case,

01:00:09.026 --> 01:00:13.780
the inputs to the smart
contracts is public,

01:00:13.781 --> 01:00:16.660
and the smart contracts

01:00:16.661 --> 01:00:19.495
essentially then
takes the inputs,

01:00:19.496 --> 01:00:20.920
and does a computation,

01:00:20.921 --> 01:00:23.560
and performs a state transition.

01:00:23.561 --> 01:00:26.605
Also, the states of
the smart contracts,

01:00:26.606 --> 01:00:30.775
in most of today's Blockchain
platform is also public.

01:00:30.776 --> 01:00:34.750
So, well, confidentiality-preserving
smart contracts

01:00:34.751 --> 01:00:39.310
execution essentially here,
everything's encrypted.

01:00:39.311 --> 01:00:41.770
So, the data is encrypted and

01:00:41.771 --> 01:00:45.530
also the state is
encrypted as well.

01:00:46.680 --> 01:00:49.720
The smart contract
essentially and

01:00:49.721 --> 01:00:52.240
that's a computation
on encrypted data,

01:00:52.241 --> 01:00:54.685
and to perform
the state transition.

01:00:54.686 --> 01:00:56.965
Also, at the same time,

01:00:56.966 --> 01:01:00.250
we want to ensure that even

01:01:00.251 --> 01:01:03.730
though the data in
states are encrypted,

01:01:03.731 --> 01:01:07.195
we still want to ensure
that we can have

01:01:07.196 --> 01:01:09.594
a proof of correctness

01:01:09.595 --> 01:01:13.430
that this state
transition is correct.

01:01:14.480 --> 01:01:18.375
&gt;&gt; So, the question is
how we actually enable

01:01:18.376 --> 01:01:22.740
this practical way of

01:01:22.741 --> 01:01:27.255
doing computation over encrypted
data and in particular,

01:01:27.256 --> 01:01:31.875
this confidentiality
preserving execution.

01:01:31.876 --> 01:01:35.400
So, this essentially, we
do this by leveraging

01:01:35.401 --> 01:01:36.780
a combination of

01:01:36.781 --> 01:01:39.960
different techniques
for secure computation.

01:01:39.961 --> 01:01:42.689
For insecure computation,
essentially,

01:01:42.690 --> 01:01:45.315
we have two main types
of approaches.

01:01:45.316 --> 01:01:48.210
The first one is
secure hardware and

01:01:48.211 --> 01:01:49.755
the advantage of secure hardware

01:01:49.756 --> 01:01:51.360
is that it's
a highly performance.

01:01:51.361 --> 01:01:54.195
Its performance is close
to native computation,

01:01:54.196 --> 01:01:57.525
and it can support for
general purpose computation.

01:01:57.526 --> 01:02:02.790
The challenge for
the other types of approach is

01:02:02.791 --> 01:02:04.980
crypto based techniques such as

01:02:04.981 --> 01:02:06.510
secure multi-party
computation and

01:02:06.511 --> 01:02:08.340
fully homomorphic
encryption, so on.

01:02:08.341 --> 01:02:10.560
The challenge for
this type of approach is

01:02:10.561 --> 01:02:13.275
that the preference overheads
can be very high.

01:02:13.276 --> 01:02:14.550
Oftentimes, its orders of

01:02:14.551 --> 01:02:17.430
magnitude higher than
native computation,

01:02:17.431 --> 01:02:19.005
and hence, it can only be used

01:02:19.006 --> 01:02:22.395
for very limited use cases.

01:02:22.396 --> 01:02:24.990
In the Oasis Blockchain platform,

01:02:24.991 --> 01:02:28.080
we actually use a combination of

01:02:28.081 --> 01:02:31.200
these different techniques
and depending

01:02:31.201 --> 01:02:35.205
on the actual use cases
of the smart contracts,

01:02:35.206 --> 01:02:39.120
we use a different
combinations of

01:02:39.121 --> 01:02:41.790
these techniques to enable

01:02:41.791 --> 01:02:45.705
confidentiality
presuming execution.

01:02:45.706 --> 01:02:48.600
So now, let me just briefly say

01:02:48.601 --> 01:02:51.795
a few words about the secure
hardware aspects.

01:02:51.796 --> 01:02:54.345
So, the secure hardware here,

01:02:54.346 --> 01:02:56.610
one obstruction is called

01:02:56.611 --> 01:02:58.680
just the execution
environments and also

01:02:58.681 --> 01:03:01.750
here we call it
the Secure Enclave.

01:03:02.360 --> 01:03:05.400
The idea of Secure
Enclave is that,

01:03:05.401 --> 01:03:08.115
here we can run a program,

01:03:08.116 --> 01:03:10.200
in this case, a smart contracts

01:03:10.201 --> 01:03:12.015
inside the Secure Enclave,

01:03:12.016 --> 01:03:16.950
and then in this case,

01:03:16.951 --> 01:03:18.420
the operating system and

01:03:18.421 --> 01:03:20.880
the application that
runs outside of

01:03:20.881 --> 01:03:22.965
the Secure Enclave will now be

01:03:22.966 --> 01:03:25.845
able to tamper with
what's around the inside,

01:03:25.846 --> 01:03:27.885
and also will not be able to

01:03:27.886 --> 01:03:29.880
see what's running
inside, and hence,

01:03:29.881 --> 01:03:31.530
the Secure Enclave
helps to provide

01:03:31.531 --> 01:03:35.155
integrity and confidentiality
of the execution.

01:03:35.156 --> 01:03:37.295
Also, at the same time,

01:03:37.296 --> 01:03:41.415
the hardware provides
a hardware-based mechanism

01:03:41.416 --> 01:03:44.100
for remote attestation
such that the remote to

01:03:44.101 --> 01:03:47.220
verify can remotely
verify what has

01:03:47.221 --> 01:03:49.365
been run inside
the Secure Enclave

01:03:49.366 --> 01:03:51.910
and its initial states.

01:03:51.911 --> 01:03:56.795
So, we have developed a research
project called Ekiden,

01:03:56.796 --> 01:04:02.270
leveraging approaches
like this and so

01:04:02.271 --> 01:04:07.320
we provide security proof
using universal composability,

01:04:07.321 --> 01:04:09.120
and that we have
developed a number

01:04:09.121 --> 01:04:11.640
of sample applications,

01:04:11.641 --> 01:04:15.120
including tiny
machinery models in

01:04:15.121 --> 01:04:18.135
healthcare domain and
smart building domain,

01:04:18.136 --> 01:04:20.595
and many other applications.

01:04:20.596 --> 01:04:24.240
So here, one of

01:04:24.241 --> 01:04:25.440
the key capabilities that were

01:04:25.441 --> 01:04:27.240
used to enable confidentiality

01:04:27.241 --> 01:04:30.074
presuming smart
contract execution

01:04:30.075 --> 01:04:34.830
as leveraging the capability
of Secure Enclaves,

01:04:34.831 --> 01:04:36.330
and in fact, the Secure Enclaves

01:04:36.331 --> 01:04:38.730
can serve as a
cornerstone security

01:04:38.731 --> 01:04:40.575
primitive even beyond

01:04:40.576 --> 01:04:43.215
the application domain
of Blockchains.

01:04:43.216 --> 01:04:45.150
Secure Enclaves can provide

01:04:45.151 --> 01:04:49.320
strong security capabilities
and can serve as

01:04:49.321 --> 01:04:52.110
a platform for building
new security applications

01:04:52.111 --> 01:04:53.820
that couldn't be
built otherwise for

01:04:53.821 --> 01:04:56.470
the same practical performance.

01:04:56.630 --> 01:04:59.460
Over the years, so

01:04:59.461 --> 01:05:01.710
all the hardware
manufacturers have recognized

01:05:01.711 --> 01:05:02.820
the importance of building

01:05:02.821 --> 01:05:04.290
secure hardware and they all

01:05:04.291 --> 01:05:06.165
have built different solutions,

01:05:06.166 --> 01:05:07.920
but however, we still have

01:05:07.921 --> 01:05:11.340
huge challenges in
building secure hardware.

01:05:11.341 --> 01:05:13.725
For example, how secure can it

01:05:13.726 --> 01:05:15.720
be under what threat models?

01:05:15.721 --> 01:05:18.975
What would you entrust
with the secure hardware?

01:05:18.976 --> 01:05:20.910
The ultimate challenge is,

01:05:20.911 --> 01:05:22.590
can we actually create

01:05:22.591 --> 01:05:25.485
a truly trustworthy
Secure Enclave as a

01:05:25.486 --> 01:05:28.650
cornerstone security
primitive that can be

01:05:28.651 --> 01:05:30.600
widely deployed and enable

01:05:30.601 --> 01:05:32.635
secure systems to
be built on top.

01:05:32.636 --> 01:05:35.840
If we can do this,
then we can truly

01:05:35.841 --> 01:05:38.540
usher the whole community into

01:05:38.541 --> 01:05:41.760
a new secure computation era.

01:05:41.800 --> 01:05:44.825
So, what's a path towards

01:05:44.826 --> 01:05:47.065
getting trustworthy
Secure Enclave.

01:05:47.066 --> 01:05:50.070
So, first we need to have
a open source design.

01:05:50.071 --> 01:05:51.960
Open source design provides

01:05:51.961 --> 01:05:54.330
transparency that's needed
for the whole community

01:05:54.331 --> 01:05:56.355
together to analyze and verify

01:05:56.356 --> 01:05:59.115
the security and correctness
of the Secure Enclave,

01:05:59.116 --> 01:06:02.880
and that can enable
much higher assurance.

01:06:02.881 --> 01:06:06.415
Also, open-source design
helps build a community.

01:06:06.416 --> 01:06:08.090
Also ideally, we would like to

01:06:08.091 --> 01:06:09.890
provide a formal verification

01:06:09.891 --> 01:06:13.325
of the Secure Enclave
and we want to

01:06:13.326 --> 01:06:16.535
ensure a secure supply
chain management for

01:06:16.536 --> 01:06:20.724
securing the
manufacturing process.

01:06:20.725 --> 01:06:22.590
So, towards this goal,

01:06:22.591 --> 01:06:27.780
we are doing a project
called the Keystone Enclave,

01:06:27.781 --> 01:06:31.665
aiming to build
open-source Secure Enclave

01:06:31.666 --> 01:06:36.210
on top of RISC-V,

01:06:36.211 --> 01:06:39.120
TEE, which is an open-source
risk architecture

01:06:39.121 --> 01:06:40.875
that's available today,

01:06:40.876 --> 01:06:43.320
and that we provide
strong memory isolation,

01:06:43.321 --> 01:06:45.795
and many other properties.

01:06:45.796 --> 01:06:48.540
I would have called to enable

01:06:48.541 --> 01:06:52.100
open-source Secure Enclave
that can really be

01:06:52.101 --> 01:06:55.170
deployed and be used and
the manufacturers by

01:06:55.171 --> 01:06:58.770
any manufacturer and be
used in the real world.

01:06:58.771 --> 01:07:05.865
RISC-V actually has been
widely adopted in industry.

01:07:05.866 --> 01:07:11.340
The RISC-V Foundation has more
than a 100 members and you

01:07:11.341 --> 01:07:13.350
can find out more
information about Keystone

01:07:13.351 --> 01:07:17.190
at our website
keystone-enclave.org,

01:07:17.191 --> 01:07:20.115
and we have a number of goals

01:07:20.116 --> 01:07:23.130
encased in the
projects to help us

01:07:23.131 --> 01:07:27.930
to finally achieve the goal

01:07:27.931 --> 01:07:31.270
of building an open-source
Secure Enclave.

01:07:31.640 --> 01:07:35.280
This is a collaboration
between Berkeley and MIT

01:07:35.281 --> 01:07:40.740
and with the other
institutions joining,

01:07:40.741 --> 01:07:45.030
and we plan to have
a first release this fall

01:07:45.031 --> 01:07:49.815
on both with deployed on FPGA,

01:07:49.816 --> 01:07:51.630
as well as, actually on

01:07:51.631 --> 01:07:58.890
the demo chip HiFive
Unleashed the RISC-V chip.

01:07:58.891 --> 01:08:02.940
So, that's the first
component technology,

01:08:02.941 --> 01:08:06.254
confidentiality-preservings
smart contract execution,

01:08:06.255 --> 01:08:08.610
and then also, quickly talk

01:08:08.611 --> 01:08:10.560
about the second component
technology for

01:08:10.561 --> 01:08:14.760
privacy-preserving analytics
and machine learning.

01:08:14.761 --> 01:08:16.950
So, while we do data analytics
and machine learning,

01:08:16.951 --> 01:08:18.795
there's a lot of privacy risks.

01:08:18.796 --> 01:08:20.325
So, for example here,

01:08:20.326 --> 01:08:23.835
there are two types of questions,

01:08:23.836 --> 01:08:25.920
one is how many trips
were taken in

01:08:25.921 --> 01:08:28.170
New York City last year?

01:08:28.171 --> 01:08:29.610
And the other one's,
how many trips

01:08:29.611 --> 01:08:31.290
did Joe take last week?

01:08:31.291 --> 01:08:32.670
So, as you can see you,

01:08:32.671 --> 01:08:35.055
one question reflects a trend and

01:08:35.056 --> 01:08:36.630
the other one actually leaks

01:08:36.631 --> 01:08:38.895
sensitive information
about the individual.

01:08:38.896 --> 01:08:43.740
But, to answer
both type of queries,

01:08:43.741 --> 01:08:45.150
you actually both need to

01:08:45.151 --> 01:08:47.960
actually have access
to data, and hence,

01:08:47.961 --> 01:08:50.075
this is an example
showing that just having

01:08:50.076 --> 01:08:53.580
access control is
insufficient to be

01:08:53.581 --> 01:08:56.190
able to protect privacy and at

01:08:56.191 --> 01:09:00.490
the same time being able to
answer queries like this.

01:09:04.040 --> 01:09:07.155
There has also been
proposals using

01:09:07.156 --> 01:09:11.280
Data Anonymization technologies
to solve this problem,

01:09:11.281 --> 01:09:14.295
but that's also insufficient

01:09:14.296 --> 01:09:16.410
Data Anonymization can reduce

01:09:16.411 --> 01:09:19.890
the utility of data and
also at the same time,

01:09:19.891 --> 01:09:23.670
it provides insufficient
privacy protection.

01:09:23.671 --> 01:09:25.530
There's a research showing

01:09:25.531 --> 01:09:27.720
that anonymized dataset actually,

01:09:27.721 --> 01:09:28.830
one is combined with

01:09:28.831 --> 01:09:31.710
the other publicly available
datasets can be

01:09:31.711 --> 01:09:36.735
used to re-identify
users and hence,

01:09:36.736 --> 01:09:40.155
leaking sensitive information
about individuals.

01:09:40.156 --> 01:09:43.050
We've also done
the recent work showing

01:09:43.051 --> 01:09:45.780
that when you want to train
a deep learning model,

01:09:45.781 --> 01:09:47.910
you actually really need
to be very, very careful.

01:09:47.911 --> 01:09:49.350
So, the question here is,

01:09:49.351 --> 01:09:52.260
do neural networks actually
remember training data?

01:09:52.261 --> 01:09:55.980
If yes, can attackers
actually extract secrets in

01:09:55.981 --> 01:09:57.885
the training data
from just simply

01:09:57.886 --> 01:10:00.074
querying the learned models.

01:10:00.075 --> 01:10:03.105
So, in collaboration
with researchers from

01:10:03.106 --> 01:10:07.005
Google in our recent paper,

01:10:07.006 --> 01:10:10.920
here is an example of one of
the key studies which show

01:10:10.921 --> 01:10:13.640
that while we train

01:10:13.641 --> 01:10:15.830
a language model on
the email dataset,

01:10:15.831 --> 01:10:18.005
in this case, it's
a public Enron dataset.

01:10:18.006 --> 01:10:21.425
The Enron email dataset
naturally contains

01:10:21.426 --> 01:10:24.095
credit card numbers and
social security numbers

01:10:24.096 --> 01:10:25.959
of actual users

01:10:25.960 --> 01:10:28.935
and we demonstrated that
attackers can actually

01:10:28.936 --> 01:10:32.160
craft attacks to them by

01:10:32.161 --> 01:10:35.655
just quering the language model
to be able to extract

01:10:35.656 --> 01:10:38.220
the credit card numbers and
social security numbers

01:10:38.221 --> 01:10:41.550
from the original
training datasets.

01:10:41.551 --> 01:10:43.950
This is an example demonstrating

01:10:43.951 --> 01:10:46.125
that even when you are training
a machine learning model,

01:10:46.126 --> 01:10:48.510
you have to be really
careful to take

01:10:48.511 --> 01:10:52.529
the correct measure to
protect users' privacy.

01:10:52.530 --> 01:10:56.520
So luckily, here we actually
have a good solution,

01:10:56.521 --> 01:11:01.335
and so we are training
a neural deep learning model,

01:11:01.336 --> 01:11:02.505
instead if we change

01:11:02.506 --> 01:11:05.535
a differentially privates
deep learning model that,

01:11:05.536 --> 01:11:10.050
in this case, both from our
proposed measure, as well as,

01:11:10.051 --> 01:11:12.630
an attack evaluation,
we demonstrate

01:11:12.631 --> 01:11:13.845
that by tuning

01:11:13.846 --> 01:11:15.870
a differentially private
deep learning model,

01:11:15.871 --> 01:11:17.760
we can provide much stronger

01:11:17.761 --> 01:11:21.030
privacy protection for the users.

01:11:21.031 --> 01:11:23.260
&gt;&gt; So, I'm running out of time.

01:11:23.261 --> 01:11:25.330
So, I won't have time
to actually go into

01:11:25.331 --> 01:11:28.165
the details about
differential privacy.

01:11:28.166 --> 01:11:30.715
So, differential privacy
at a high level is

01:11:30.716 --> 01:11:34.555
essentially a formal
privacy notion that

01:11:34.556 --> 01:11:37.255
helps to measure for

01:11:37.256 --> 01:11:41.500
algorithm and the editor
analytics algorithm,

01:11:41.501 --> 01:11:44.575
or machine learning
algorithm whether it can

01:11:44.576 --> 01:11:47.770
distinguish whether
a particular user's data has

01:11:47.771 --> 01:11:50.695
been used in producing

01:11:50.696 --> 01:11:52.330
the data analytics results

01:11:52.331 --> 01:11:54.685
or tuning the machine
learning model.

01:11:54.686 --> 01:11:58.780
So, differential privacy provides

01:11:58.781 --> 01:12:01.300
a very strong notion for

01:12:01.301 --> 01:12:03.130
protecting user's privacy in

01:12:03.131 --> 01:12:05.335
data analytics and
machine learning.

01:12:05.336 --> 01:12:08.845
There has been
very limited real-world use

01:12:08.846 --> 01:12:10.209
of differential privacy

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:12.580
including Google and Apple

01:12:12.581 --> 01:12:15.580
use differential privacy
in very limited setting.

01:12:15.581 --> 01:12:18.100
There's no previous
real-world's deployments of

01:12:18.101 --> 01:12:21.620
differential privacy for
general-purpose analytics.

01:12:22.470 --> 01:12:24.970
There are a number
of challenges for

01:12:24.971 --> 01:12:28.900
deploying differential privacy in

01:12:28.901 --> 01:12:31.310
practice in the real world

01:12:31.620 --> 01:12:35.185
including usability
for non-experts,

01:12:35.186 --> 01:12:37.150
broad support for
analytics queries

01:12:37.151 --> 01:12:38.410
in machine learning and

01:12:38.411 --> 01:12:42.625
also easy integration with
existing data environments.

01:12:42.626 --> 01:12:45.220
There's no previous system

01:12:45.221 --> 01:12:46.825
that addresses all these issues.

01:12:46.826 --> 01:12:48.670
In collaboration
with Uber we have

01:12:48.671 --> 01:12:51.625
developed new systems to address

01:12:51.626 --> 01:12:58.390
these challenges and in
our system Chorus and Optio,

01:12:58.391 --> 01:13:01.390
we essentially develop
techniques and

01:13:01.391 --> 01:13:05.230
tools to automatically
rewrite data analytics

01:13:05.231 --> 01:13:09.085
and machine learning
pipelines to enforce

01:13:09.086 --> 01:13:11.590
differential privacy as well as

01:13:11.591 --> 01:13:16.210
other desired privacy
requirements.

01:13:16.211 --> 01:13:20.530
Some of our technology has
already been deployed at

01:13:20.531 --> 01:13:24.159
Uber for protecting privacy

01:13:24.160 --> 01:13:27.320
in their internal data analytics.

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:31.300
So, putting together
confidentiality preserving

01:13:31.301 --> 01:13:33.160
smart contract
execution and privacy

01:13:33.161 --> 01:13:36.435
preserving data analytics
and machine learning.

01:13:36.436 --> 01:13:41.890
So, this enables the privacy
preserving smart contracts.

01:13:42.150 --> 01:13:44.830
Now, let me just
briefly talk about

01:13:44.831 --> 01:13:46.915
the third component technology

01:13:46.916 --> 01:13:49.870
as scalable smart
contract execution.

01:13:49.871 --> 01:13:52.540
Again, I don't have
a lot of time left

01:13:52.541 --> 01:13:55.060
so let me just go over
this very quickly.

01:13:55.061 --> 01:13:56.830
So, typically when people

01:13:56.831 --> 01:13:59.545
talk about Blockchain
scalability,

01:13:59.546 --> 01:14:01.090
one thing that people
immediately think

01:14:01.091 --> 01:14:03.265
about is Blockchain sharding.

01:14:03.266 --> 01:14:07.135
It's like you have one lane
of cars but now you

01:14:07.136 --> 01:14:09.700
have many lanes in parallel

01:14:09.701 --> 01:14:13.435
to improve the throughput
of the traffic.

01:14:13.436 --> 01:14:15.220
However, this type of approach

01:14:15.221 --> 01:14:16.720
is good for high-throughput of

01:14:16.721 --> 01:14:20.604
simple transactions but however,

01:14:20.605 --> 01:14:24.250
for the Blockchain applications
that we want to build

01:14:24.251 --> 01:14:27.070
these are much more

01:14:27.071 --> 01:14:29.140
complex and so the difference

01:14:29.141 --> 01:14:32.140
smart contracts can also
depend on each other.

01:14:32.141 --> 01:14:37.750
So, to enable scalability
in particular scalability

01:14:37.751 --> 01:14:40.360
for complex smart
contract execution,

01:14:40.361 --> 01:14:45.250
we have designed and developed

01:14:45.251 --> 01:14:48.610
a different Blockchain
architecture

01:14:48.611 --> 01:14:53.120
than previous Blockchain
architectures.

01:14:54.030 --> 01:14:59.950
In particular by separating
out execution from consensus.

01:14:59.951 --> 01:15:03.100
By decoupling the
different functions that

01:15:03.101 --> 01:15:06.865
a Blockchain is providing.

01:15:06.866 --> 01:15:11.860
So, our design is inspired by

01:15:11.861 --> 01:15:15.280
a few key observations
of the limitations and

01:15:15.281 --> 01:15:19.210
challenges in today's
Blockchain approaches.

01:15:19.211 --> 01:15:23.410
So first, a Blockchain
platform essentially

01:15:23.411 --> 01:15:27.760
provides three main
functionalities consensus,

01:15:27.761 --> 01:15:29.155
storage and compute.

01:15:29.156 --> 01:15:31.360
Our functions in most of

01:15:31.361 --> 01:15:33.070
today's Blockchain platforms or

01:15:33.071 --> 01:15:35.260
the three functionalities
are coupled

01:15:35.261 --> 01:15:37.375
together and hence makes it

01:15:37.376 --> 01:15:40.090
really difficult to
scale and also hear

01:15:40.091 --> 01:15:43.090
consensus as
the slow operation and

01:15:43.091 --> 01:15:48.010
is often the key bottleneck.

01:15:48.330 --> 01:15:50.830
When we do sharding,

01:15:50.831 --> 01:15:53.755
coordination also is expensive

01:15:53.756 --> 01:15:57.775
and can limit
the scalability as well.

01:15:57.776 --> 01:16:01.795
So, based on these observations

01:16:01.796 --> 01:16:05.260
and to address these challenges

01:16:05.261 --> 01:16:10.375
we propose a new
Blockchain architecture

01:16:10.376 --> 01:16:13.855
for what we call separating
execution from consensus.

01:16:13.856 --> 01:16:15.745
Where we actually decouple

01:16:15.746 --> 01:16:18.775
these different functionalities
of a Blockchain platform,

01:16:18.776 --> 01:16:21.970
where we separate out
the consensus layer,

01:16:21.971 --> 01:16:23.770
the storage layer and
the compute layer.

01:16:23.771 --> 01:16:30.625
Where the computer executes

01:16:30.626 --> 01:16:33.264
the smart contracts and

01:16:33.265 --> 01:16:35.440
the storage layer
persist the state and

01:16:35.441 --> 01:16:38.900
the consensus layer agrees
on the state transitions.

01:16:42.060 --> 01:16:45.480
So, I think here there's
limited time so I

01:16:45.481 --> 01:16:49.660
probably won't have time to
go into the details here.

01:16:49.661 --> 01:16:52.105
So, essentially to separate

01:16:52.106 --> 01:16:54.685
execution from a consensus we-

01:16:54.686 --> 01:17:00.115
so again the compute layer

01:17:00.116 --> 01:17:01.720
does the computation and in

01:17:01.721 --> 01:17:04.450
this case actually the compute
nodes are stateless so

01:17:04.451 --> 01:17:06.729
no consensus protocols
are necessary

01:17:06.730 --> 01:17:10.510
and they can optimistically
execute the transactions.

01:17:10.511 --> 01:17:14.680
In this case the failures
have no effect on the state,

01:17:14.681 --> 01:17:18.160
and the states are persistence to

01:17:18.161 --> 01:17:21.670
the storage layer
where the blocks

01:17:21.671 --> 01:17:23.920
are persisted and replicated and

01:17:23.921 --> 01:17:25.390
again no consensus protocol are

01:17:25.391 --> 01:17:28.920
necessary and writes in
this case are idempotent.

01:17:28.921 --> 01:17:32.605
Finally, the state transitions

01:17:32.606 --> 01:17:35.950
are committed to the consensus
layer and here we use

01:17:35.951 --> 01:17:40.960
verifiable computation to
verify the correctness of

01:17:40.961 --> 01:17:44.290
the computation and
the consensus layer

01:17:44.291 --> 01:17:46.600
essentially tracks
the state transitions

01:17:46.601 --> 01:17:48.460
and the transaction ordering.

01:17:48.461 --> 01:17:51.220
Then we use different types
of techniques for

01:17:51.221 --> 01:17:54.100
verifiable computing
including majority votes,

01:17:54.101 --> 01:18:00.369
and a new approach that we
developed and discrepancy

01:18:00.370 --> 01:18:02.185
detection and we can also use

01:18:02.186 --> 01:18:03.910
other methods such as

01:18:03.911 --> 01:18:07.435
zero-knowledge proofs for
verifiable computing.

01:18:07.436 --> 01:18:10.390
So, putting all these
technologies together,

01:18:10.391 --> 01:18:14.620
we enable privacy preserving
smart contracts as scale.

01:18:14.621 --> 01:18:16.795
Here is one of the applications

01:18:16.796 --> 01:18:18.805
that we have been building,

01:18:18.806 --> 01:18:23.575
together with
other collaborators from

01:18:23.576 --> 01:18:29.250
ETH and doctors from Stanford
as well as app developers.

01:18:29.251 --> 01:18:31.355
&gt;&gt; An application called Kara,

01:18:31.356 --> 01:18:33.350
a privacy-preserving
tokenized data

01:18:33.351 --> 01:18:35.690
markets for medical data.

01:18:35.691 --> 01:18:40.970
So, medical data is locked in
data silos and the goal of

01:18:40.971 --> 01:18:43.460
Kara is to incentivize
doctors and patients

01:18:43.461 --> 01:18:47.220
to share data and improve
medical research.

01:18:47.470 --> 01:18:51.740
We want to provide an easy, fast,

01:18:51.741 --> 01:18:55.920
and secure way for
doctors and patients to

01:18:56.710 --> 01:19:00.365
essentially upload
their data to the platform

01:19:00.366 --> 01:19:04.175
and share their data and they
can earn rewards for it,

01:19:04.176 --> 01:19:08.670
while at the same time,
preserving their privacy.

01:19:10.180 --> 01:19:14.105
By utilizing the
privacy-preserving smart contracts

01:19:14.106 --> 01:19:16.175
on top of the Oasis Blockchain,

01:19:16.176 --> 01:19:19.640
the medical researchers can write

01:19:19.641 --> 01:19:22.010
their privacy-preserving
smart contracts

01:19:22.011 --> 01:19:23.720
and within the smart contracts,

01:19:23.721 --> 01:19:25.820
it can contain code

01:19:25.821 --> 01:19:27.995
for training machine
learning models, as well as,

01:19:27.996 --> 01:19:30.050
serving machine learning
models and also

01:19:30.051 --> 01:19:31.130
the smart contracts can

01:19:31.131 --> 01:19:33.170
specify terms of
use, for example,

01:19:33.171 --> 01:19:37.280
how users may get

01:19:37.281 --> 01:19:39.950
compensated for
contributing their data

01:19:39.951 --> 01:19:41.855
to the smart contract.

01:19:41.856 --> 01:19:44.585
Also, users and patients,

01:19:44.586 --> 01:19:46.430
they can also specify

01:19:46.431 --> 01:19:49.580
their own privacy requirements
for their data and

01:19:49.581 --> 01:19:51.980
if they agree to
the terms of use of

01:19:51.981 --> 01:19:55.220
the smart contract developed
by the medical researchers,

01:19:55.221 --> 01:19:57.200
then they can contribute
their data to

01:19:57.201 --> 01:20:02.015
the smart contracts developed
by the medical researcher.

01:20:02.016 --> 01:20:04.370
Once the smart contract
collects enough data,

01:20:04.371 --> 01:20:07.430
then it can train machine
learning models over the data

01:20:07.431 --> 01:20:12.020
and then this way can
help medical researchers

01:20:12.021 --> 01:20:15.440
and pharmaceutical
companies to do

01:20:15.441 --> 01:20:17.525
their medical research and to

01:20:17.526 --> 01:20:20.465
help find better cures
for diseases.

01:20:20.466 --> 01:20:23.420
With this approach,
it can significantly

01:20:23.421 --> 01:20:26.060
reduce the friction for
medical researchers and

01:20:26.061 --> 01:20:29.600
pharmaceutical companies to
get access to data to be able

01:20:29.601 --> 01:20:34.460
to train machine learning
models for their work.

01:20:34.461 --> 01:20:36.905
This is just one of

01:20:36.906 --> 01:20:40.325
many examples for
the different use cases that

01:20:40.326 --> 01:20:42.395
a Blockchain platform
like this can

01:20:42.396 --> 01:20:45.545
enable by providing both
privacy protection,

01:20:45.546 --> 01:20:49.290
as well as, scalability.

01:20:49.330 --> 01:20:53.705
We just launched
Oasis Labs recently,

01:20:53.706 --> 01:20:58.970
we raised $45 million and
we have a lot to build,

01:20:58.971 --> 01:21:02.674
we actually also have
launched our private Testnet,

01:21:02.675 --> 01:21:06.245
and we're inviting
developers to join

01:21:06.246 --> 01:21:08.360
our premier Testnet to build

01:21:08.361 --> 01:21:12.710
applications and new technologies
on top of our platform.

01:21:12.711 --> 01:21:16.790
So, at Oasis Labs, we're
building privacy-first,

01:21:16.791 --> 01:21:21.605
high performance Cloud computing
platform on Blockchain.

01:21:21.606 --> 01:21:25.160
New computing paradigm and
computing platform that

01:21:25.161 --> 01:21:28.924
has a unique combination
of set-up properties,

01:21:28.925 --> 01:21:32.345
privacy protection,
scalability, as well as,

01:21:32.346 --> 01:21:35.510
without needing to rely
on any central party.

01:21:35.511 --> 01:21:38.420
We hope that this
technology can really

01:21:38.421 --> 01:21:42.665
help enable completely
new type of applications

01:21:42.666 --> 01:21:45.860
to be built that
couldn't be built today

01:21:45.861 --> 01:21:51.170
and for more information
you can go to the website.

01:21:51.171 --> 01:22:00.755
Thank you. Questions?

01:22:00.756 --> 01:22:02.975
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm going back to
your original example.

01:22:02.976 --> 01:22:04.280
The original example
is there were

01:22:04.281 --> 01:22:05.870
two banks and you wanted to

01:22:05.871 --> 01:22:07.490
build a machine learning model

01:22:07.491 --> 01:22:08.910
that combined
the datasets. Right?

01:22:08.911 --> 01:22:09.955
&gt;&gt; Multiple banks.

01:22:09.956 --> 01:22:12.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, multiple banks.
So, my question is can it

01:22:12.571 --> 01:22:15.215
come to solve this problem by
just using secure hardware?

01:22:15.216 --> 01:22:16.795
What is the Blockchain
connection?

01:22:16.796 --> 01:22:19.150
It seems like you're
unioning datasets,

01:22:19.151 --> 01:22:20.950
so just encrypt the data,

01:22:20.951 --> 01:22:22.835
register keys, and
secure hardware and

01:22:22.836 --> 01:22:25.180
maybe run a R program
inside hardware.

01:22:25.181 --> 01:22:25.740
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:22:25.741 --> 01:22:27.110
&gt;&gt; So, what is
the Blockchain connection?

01:22:27.111 --> 01:22:28.940
Are you afraid of someone
tampering the data?

01:22:28.941 --> 01:22:30.170
Why do you need Blockchains?

01:22:30.171 --> 01:22:32.360
&gt;&gt; So, usually when
you use Blockchain as

01:22:32.361 --> 01:22:34.550
that you don't want to rely
on your central party.

01:22:34.551 --> 01:22:35.780
So, then you don't need

01:22:35.781 --> 01:22:38.730
to say like who is operating
that, so, essentially,

01:22:38.731 --> 01:22:39.960
you have a Blockchain platform

01:22:39.961 --> 01:22:43.065
that people don't even
need to agree on.

01:22:43.066 --> 01:22:44.595
Like who should be

01:22:44.596 --> 01:22:47.310
trusted and who should
be operating this?

01:22:47.311 --> 01:22:50.550
So, it's just right
a platform that without

01:22:50.551 --> 01:22:52.530
relying on any central party

01:22:52.531 --> 01:22:54.735
and in particular in this case,

01:22:54.736 --> 01:22:57.480
like here I just gave
an example of a few banks,

01:22:57.481 --> 01:23:00.440
but you can imagine that in
other scenarios you can have

01:23:00.441 --> 01:23:02.000
many parties and you
are now going to

01:23:02.001 --> 01:23:03.930
have all of them to agree,

01:23:03.931 --> 01:23:06.740
to reach agreements on
like who they want to

01:23:06.741 --> 01:23:07.985
trust and who should be

01:23:07.986 --> 01:23:09.980
coordinating and running
all these things.

01:23:09.981 --> 01:23:11.825
So, with the Blockchain,
it just helps

01:23:11.826 --> 01:23:16.190
to again removing the reliance
on any such party.

01:23:16.191 --> 01:23:17.390
&gt;&gt; Another question, but

01:23:17.391 --> 01:23:18.740
the smart contract code

01:23:18.741 --> 01:23:20.375
itself that the
register number object.

01:23:20.376 --> 01:23:22.935
That'll still be public that
is not encrypted, right?

01:23:22.936 --> 01:23:24.555
&gt;&gt; That's a very
good question, so

01:23:24.556 --> 01:23:26.445
in our platform
actually this will

01:23:26.446 --> 01:23:30.045
be a choice of the developers.

01:23:30.046 --> 01:23:31.635
So, the developer can specify

01:23:31.636 --> 01:23:33.270
either they want
their smart contract to be

01:23:33.271 --> 01:23:36.935
public or they can
be fully private.

01:23:36.936 --> 01:23:39.570
Yeah, in which case,
it will be encrypted.

01:23:46.900 --> 01:23:51.950
&gt;&gt; So, there's also this company
Enigma from MIT and I

01:23:51.951 --> 01:23:54.410
was reading their white
paper and it seems

01:23:54.411 --> 01:23:55.690
like they were also

01:23:55.691 --> 01:23:57.460
trying to solve
something very similar,

01:23:57.461 --> 01:24:00.245
so maybe if you could say

01:24:00.246 --> 01:24:01.810
something comparing
the two approaches

01:24:01.811 --> 01:24:04.085
that would be really helpful.

01:24:04.086 --> 01:24:08.945
&gt;&gt; I think for privacy-preserving
smart contracts,

01:24:08.946 --> 01:24:14.360
in general, people
understand its importance.

01:24:14.361 --> 01:24:17.375
Actually, I think it's
a very good problem for

01:24:17.376 --> 01:24:19.950
many people to solve and
I think it'll be great

01:24:19.951 --> 01:24:22.060
to see the whole community
come together

01:24:22.061 --> 01:24:24.920
to overcome
this problem and solve

01:24:24.921 --> 01:24:27.830
this problem as
our academic paper is

01:24:27.831 --> 01:24:30.260
the first paper in a community
that came out that

01:24:30.261 --> 01:24:32.965
actually develop
technology on this,

01:24:32.966 --> 01:24:37.940
and again I think it's
a very important problem,

01:24:37.941 --> 01:24:41.475
we want to encourage
the whole community

01:24:41.476 --> 01:24:43.510
to actually work on this.

01:24:44.590 --> 01:24:47.305
&gt;&gt; I had one follow-up question.

01:24:47.306 --> 01:24:49.500
So, in one of your earlier slides

01:24:49.501 --> 01:24:51.990
you had approaches, right?

01:24:51.991 --> 01:24:53.480
You were using secure hardware,

01:24:53.481 --> 01:24:55.110
using multiparty
computation, you are

01:24:55.111 --> 01:24:57.690
using zero-knowledge proofs
and potentially others.

01:24:57.691 --> 01:24:59.260
So, my question is like let's

01:24:59.261 --> 01:25:00.970
assume that secured hardware
is really secured.

01:25:00.971 --> 01:25:02.100
There I know there
are issues with

01:25:02.101 --> 01:25:04.630
that like what do you
need other approaches

01:25:04.631 --> 01:25:07.090
because won't it be
able to solve like say

01:25:07.091 --> 01:25:10.910
multi-party competition if
you have a secured hardware?

01:25:10.911 --> 01:25:12.815
&gt;&gt; Okay, yes that's
a very good question.

01:25:12.816 --> 01:25:15.620
So, essentially one
of the main goals of

01:25:15.621 --> 01:25:18.350
the Oasis platform with this
privacy-preserving smart

01:25:18.351 --> 01:25:20.510
contracts primitive
is that we want

01:25:20.511 --> 01:25:23.570
to offer to make it easier for

01:25:23.571 --> 01:25:25.400
application developers to use

01:25:25.401 --> 01:25:27.420
state of the art security and

01:25:27.421 --> 01:25:31.470
privacy technologies without
needing to be a experts,

01:25:31.471 --> 01:25:35.280
and also in this case
essentially we provide

01:25:35.281 --> 01:25:37.985
a unified secure
computing framework

01:25:37.986 --> 01:25:41.300
to leverage a combination

01:25:41.301 --> 01:25:42.980
of these different technologies,

01:25:42.981 --> 01:25:44.780
and yes, if you're really choose

01:25:44.781 --> 01:25:46.585
to fully trust
the secured hardware,

01:25:46.586 --> 01:25:48.925
then essentially you
don't need to use the

01:25:48.926 --> 01:25:52.355
other and like the crypto
based techniques, but however,

01:25:52.356 --> 01:25:56.840
depending on the actual use case

01:25:56.841 --> 01:25:59.450
and also depending on
developers preference.

01:25:59.451 --> 01:26:01.445
So, some developers they

01:26:01.446 --> 01:26:04.340
just want to use
crypto based techniques and if

01:26:04.341 --> 01:26:07.285
their application logic
is simple enough

01:26:07.286 --> 01:26:08.995
and they don't care

01:26:08.996 --> 01:26:10.950
too much about
the performance overheads,

01:26:10.951 --> 01:26:13.155
then we are happy to offer

01:26:13.156 --> 01:26:17.150
them the choice of not
relying on secure hardware.

01:26:19.250 --> 01:26:22.665
&gt;&gt; Any other questions?
Okay, so let's thank

01:26:22.666 --> 01:26:28.200
the speaker again.
Thanks, everybody.

